{"id": "191", "pred": "Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "raw_output": "Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "gt": "To attach multiple picture frames together, you can use small hinges to connect the frames. Lay the frames beside each other to determine the desired arrangement. For two frames, use identical ones, and for three frames, either choose three identical frames or use a larger one as the middle frame. Mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides of the frames, ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges. Attach the hinges to securely join the frames.", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 5.743, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 12, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [30485, 1378, 2613, 79834, 311, 279, 20913, 11067, 315, 279, 14087, 13], "logp_list": [-0.9318, -0.23, -0.0381, -0.0002, -0.0078, -0.0023, -0.3973, -0.0006, -0.0013, -0.0068, -0.0096, -0.1204], "cumulative_logprob": -1.7462, "norm_logp": -0.1455, "input_len": 471, "output_tokens": 12, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.95, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "197", "pred": "Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed assets for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax deductible payments.", "raw_output": "Leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed assets for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax deductible payments.", "gt": "Leasing refers to a process in which a firm obtains the use of fixed assets by making contractual, periodic, and tax-deductible payments to the owner of those assets. The lessee is the party receiving the services or assets under the lease contract, while the lessor is the owner of the assets.", "question": "leasing meaning", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 6.986, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 34, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [2304, 4422, 374, 264, 1882, 553, 892, 264, 7474, 646, 6851, 279, 990, 315, 264, 3654, 8356, 11770, 369, 892, 432, 1969, 2291, 264, 4013, 315, 75443, 11, 38345, 11, 3742, 77812, 14173, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4864, -0.0075, -0.3873, -0.0979, -0.3976, -0.1682, -0.0002, -0.0057, -0.0026, -0.0245, -0.0098, -0.0023, -0.0026, -0.0008, -0.0751, -0.0054, -0.0075, -0.0144, -0.0035, -0.0023, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0013, -0.0007, -0.0029, -0.0011, -0.0054, -0.0003, -0.0318], "cumulative_logprob": -1.7476, "norm_logp": -0.0514, "input_len": 376, "output_tokens": 34, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.86, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "205", "pred": "chard is a beet that", "raw_output": "chard is a beet that", "gt": "Chard is a vegetable that has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sautéed. Chard is in the same subspecies as beetroot and is cultivated for leaf production. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stems are slightly tougher. Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a minimum amount of time.", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 1.798, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 6, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [331, 567, 374, 264, 48012, 429], "logp_list": [-0.8295, -0.0001, -0.7496, -0.1023, -0.498, -0.5266], "cumulative_logprob": -2.7061, "norm_logp": -0.451, "input_len": 423, "output_tokens": 6, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.12, "GAVIE-Rel": 2.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "210", "pred": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. In other words", "raw_output": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. In other words", "gt": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, meaning all the atoms in an element have the same number of protons. Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and so retain their own identity. The individual components of a mixture can be physically separated from each other. Mixtures have variable compositions and show the properties of their constituent substances. Elements and compounds just mix together to form a mixture and no new compound is formed.", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 4.916, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 23, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [11868, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 323, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 13, 758, 1008, 4244], "logp_list": [-1.1642, -0.6867, -0.3932, -0.012, -0.0009, -0.0032, -0.0021, -0.0132, -0.0009, -0.0024, -0.0945, -0.0527, -0.0017, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0205, -0.3906, -0.1473, -0.1635], "cumulative_logprob": -3.1521, "norm_logp": -0.137, "input_len": 415, "output_tokens": 23, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.58, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 2.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "214", "pred": "To wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n\n1. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adher", "raw_output": "To wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n\n1. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adher", "gt": "To properly wash wine glasses, you should scrub them by hand as it's the safest cleaning method. First, lay a towel in the sink before filling it with hot water. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage. Use a small amount of detergent only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim. To clean the inside of a glass, use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles. If hot water and mild detergent can’t get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adhering to the glass. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem. After rinsing thoroughly with hot water, dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.", "question": "how to wash wine glasses properly", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 13.381, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 65, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 11369, 13078, 28147, 10277, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1416, 4017, 3015, 323, 23034, 90134, 646, 944, 633, 697, 28147, 4240, 11, 990, 27815, 38862, 311, 4057, 894, 51396, 13078, 48132, 13, 51032, 3948, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 38862, 448, 4017, 3015, 323, 1077, 432, 2498, 304, 279, 8991, 369, 264, 2421, 4420, 13, 576, 38862, 686, 72639, 894, 32147, 13078, 34951], "logp_list": [-0.9255, -0.5418, -0.0245, -0.0002, -0.0083, -0.0197, -1.0623, -0.0433, -0.0405, -0.5628, -0.0505, -0.0032, -1.1422, -0.0689, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0028, -0.0001, -0.1504, -0.0165, -0.1155, -0.0662, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0242, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0118, -0.0002, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.3788, -0.0237, -0.0001, -0.0029, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0014, -0.0044, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0123, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0031, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.055, -0.0333, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0004, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.0239], "cumulative_logprob": -5.4348, "norm_logp": -0.0836, "input_len": 328, "output_tokens": 65, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.66, "GAVIE-Rel": 1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "223", "pred": "Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet. Step 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arm. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm. Step 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The then end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If", "raw_output": "Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet. Step 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arm. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm. Step 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The then end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If", "gt": "Based on the given passages, to fix a lift valve in a toilet, you should follow these steps:\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms and remove the broken lift chain.\n3. Install a new flapper valve and chain.\n4. Adjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n5. Cut off any excess lift chain to prevent tangling.\n6. Push the solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle and secure it tightly.\n7. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm closest to the edge.\n8. Use pliers to securely fasten the chain.\n9. Turn the water valve back on.\n10. Test the flush of the toilet and adjust the chain if needed.\n\nNote: The given passages provide step-by-step instructions for fixing a lift valve in a toilet.", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 43.676, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 212, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 1417, 27440, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 311, 279, 26214, 13, 14822, 220, 17, 25, 10783, 279, 2310, 1320, 3106, 31430, 553, 63702, 432, 1007, 279, 33639, 6773, 13, 10783, 1128, 594, 2115, 315, 279, 10865, 11893, 8781, 504, 279, 11893, 6773, 13, 14822, 220, 19, 25, 5264, 279, 3015, 31430, 311, 5155, 279, 12896, 13, 57626, 279, 26214, 311, 1779, 369, 4396, 5666, 13, 1416, 4362, 11, 1349, 4250, 279, 11893, 8781, 553, 7218, 279, 9317, 12327, 705, 476, 1495, 264, 5625, 315, 7746, 13, 3197, 498, 3129, 19527, 11, 3931, 1007, 279, 13623, 11893, 8781, 311, 5358, 432, 504, 3709, 92841, 13, 22950, 279, 2613, 6437, 835, 315, 279, 26214, 11893, 6773, 1119, 279, 18198, 3705, 13, 1416, 264, 21966, 374, 5230, 448, 697, 949, 11, 21966, 432, 304, 38969, 13, 22950, 432, 3080, 1052, 374, 902, 803, 2968, 323, 432, 374, 9767, 13, 576, 1221, 835, 686, 14678, 25580, 304, 279, 3720, 13, 13015, 279, 8781, 311, 279, 4843, 14178, 389, 279, 11893, 6773, 13, 1096, 374, 279, 14178, 18093, 311, 279, 315, 279, 11893, 6773, 13, 5443, 279, 625, 4813, 311, 1281, 2704, 432, 374, 51023, 4937, 6758, 13, 11999, 279, 3015, 31430, 1182, 389, 323, 1273, 700, 279, 18198, 315, 279, 26214, 13, 1416], "logp_list": [-1.2286, -0.0477, -0.1, -0.0074, -0.4077, -0.0011, -0.0048, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.001, -0.0034, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.4914, -0.1666, -0.0004, -0.0049, -0.0006, -0.0111, -0.0008, -0.0015, -0.009, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0087, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0623, -0.0028, -0.017, -0.0267, -0.007, -0.0372, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0278, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0271, -0.2391, -0.0013, -0.3552, -0.0005, -0.0031, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0021, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0168, -0.0179, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0074, -0.0334, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.1538, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0111, -0.0002, -0.3155, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0016, -0.0064, -0.5078, -0.0001, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0037, -0.0063, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0034, -0.0001, -0.0035, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0108, -0.5701, -0.0042, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0079, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0094, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0114, -0.0059, -0.0463, -0.1223, -0.367, -0.0178, -0.0119, -0.0009, -0.0013, -0.0278, -0.0007, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0012, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0014, -0.0259, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0019, -0.1076, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0023, -0.0003, -0.0015, -0.0132, -0