{"id": "297", "pred": "Examples of Potential Energy and Kinetic Energy. Potential energy is that energy stored in any body whereas kinetic energy is the energy a body possesses by its motion. We see many a activities across us like a car moves, a ball rolls, river flows, an athlete runs, rides in amusement park, a satellite in orbit etc.", "raw_output": "Examples of Potential Energy and Kinetic Energy. Potential energy is that energy stored in any body whereas kinetic energy is the energy a body possesses by its motion. We see many a activities across us like a car moves, a ball rolls, river flows, an athlete runs, rides in amusement park, a satellite in orbit etc.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, examples of potential energy include an object's gravitational potential energy due to its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy stored in objects due to their deformation. Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane's kinetic energy in flight due to its large mass and fast velocity, a baseball's kinetic energy due to its fast velocity despite its small mass, and the kinetic energy possessed by objects in motion such as cars, balls, rivers, athletes, amusement park rides, and satellites in orbit.", "question": "what are examples of potential and kinetic energy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 18.173, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 65, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [40381, 315, 53957, 12354, 323, 30891, 5298, 12354, 13, 53957, 4802, 374, 429, 4802, 9768, 304, 894, 2487, 19853, 70323, 4802, 374, 279, 4802, 264, 2487, 49226, 553, 1181, 11379, 13, 1205, 1490, 1657, 264, 7488, 3941, 601, 1075, 264, 1803, 10797, 11, 264, 4935, 27373, 11, 14796, 27455, 11, 458, 33780, 8473, 11, 31227, 304, 61368, 6118, 11, 264, 23210, 304, 26507, 4992, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4453, -0.0165, -1.0925, -0.0687, -1.2117, -0.0052, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.73, -0.1346, -0.0098, -0.0041, -0.0284, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0003, -0.006, -0.0017, -0.0225, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0025, -0.0002, -0.0112, -0.0002, -0.0091, -0.0035, -0.0021, -0.0001, -0.0154, -0.3482, -0.0017, -0.0015, -0.1167, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0017, -0.0013, -0.0002, -0.0658, -0.4847, -0.0262, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0195, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.0032, -0.0001, -0.0055, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0013, -0.0163, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0473, -0.0368], "cumulative_logprob": -5.0122, "norm_logp": -0.0771, "input_len": 285, "output_tokens": 65, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.95, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "301", "pred": "A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. A pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot.", "raw_output": "A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. A pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how you can make a hot water heater circulate:\n\n1. Install a recirculating pump: As described in passage 3, a recirculating pump can rapidly pull hot water from the water heater and send cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused.\n2. Use a demand-based system: According to passage 3, demand systems can be controlled by the push of a button, a timer, or motion sensor. This means that the pump will only run when there is a demand for hot water, rather than constantly running.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide specific instructions on how to make a hot water heater circulate, but rather describe the concept of circulation and the different types of systems used to achieve it.", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 8.005, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 38, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 4017, 3015, 34755, 13834, 21308, 7010, 3015, 304, 279, 31175, 1182, 311, 279, 3015, 42238, 1526, 264, 470, 1555, 13, 362, 13834, 4225, 23156, 419, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 438, 4362, 311, 2506, 432, 4017, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3636, -0.1354, -0.0013, -0.1394, -0.0008, -0.0307, -0.0047, -0.0001, -0.023, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0246, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.1224, -0.5183, -0.0003, -0.005, -0.0001, -0.1771, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0204], "cumulative_logprob": -2.5766, "norm_logp": -0.0678, "input_len": 298, "output_tokens": 38, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 23.73, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "310", "pred": "$100-$500 per square foot", "raw_output": "$100-$500 per square foot", "gt": "Hiring a remodeling contractor to build a second floor addition can cost between $100-$300 a square foot for a typical addition, but the cost can go as high as $400-$500 per square foot depending on the complexity, quality of materials and local labor rates. Building a second floor can cost at least 20 percent more than building the same square footage at ground level.", "question": "avg cost to install a second floor in a house", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 2.843, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 11, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [3, 16, 15, 15, 21389, 20, 15, 15, 817, 9334, 4478], "logp_list": [-0.2286, -0.8144, -0.1109, -0.0024, -0.052, -0.529, -0.0009, -0.0006, -1.1084, -0.0016, -0.0028], "cumulative_logprob": -2.8516, "norm_logp": -0.2592, "input_len": 521, "output_tokens": 11, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 12.24, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "321", "pred": "The effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of your lifestyle. Everything you do has an impact --- positive or negative --- on the environment. That's the concept behind your carbon footprint", "raw_output": "The effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of your lifestyle. Everything you do has an impact --- positive or negative --- on the environment. That's the concept behind your carbon footprint", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of an individual's lifestyle and daily activities, specifically the amount of greenhouse gases produced, such as carbon dioxide, as a result of those activities. The carbon footprint is typically measured in tons of CO2 released into the atmosphere. The passages also mention that reducing one's carbon footprint can have a significant impact on the environment, even with seemingly small changes. Additionally, the average carbon footprint is mentioned to be around 6 tons per year, with a higher amount in the US due to automobile emissions.", "question": "what is the effect of carbon footprint", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.881, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 38, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 2456, 315, 12499, 42872, 374, 311, 6629, 279, 12158, 5421, 315, 697, 18899, 13, 20094, 498, 653, 702, 458, 5421, 12448, 6785, 476, 8225, 12448, 389, 279, 4573, 13, 2938, 594, 279, 7286, 4815, 697, 12499, 42872], "logp_list": [-0.2729, -0.5008, -0.0012, -0.4579, -0.0008, -0.2654, -0.7186, -0.0289, -0.0056, -0.0437, -0.001, -0.0013, -0.7877, -0.0014, -0.2513, -0.9309, -0.0102, -0.0009, -0.2073, -0.0083, -0.0009, -1.0151, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0683, -1.0988, -0.0101, -0.0018, -0.0, -0.0034, -0.0114, -0.0002, -0.0], "cumulative_logprob": -6.7085, "norm_logp": -0.1765, "input_len": 367, "output_tokens": 38, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.58, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "331", "pred": "Boil until tender, then transfer to an ice water bath. When cool, peel off skin. (15 minutes to cook, 5 seconds to peel each cooked potato)", "raw_output": "Boil until tender, then transfer to an ice water bath. When cool, peel off skin. (15 minutes to cook, 5 seconds to peel each cooked potato)", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to boil potatoes for easy peeling:\n\n1. Bring a pot of water to a boil.\n2. Score your potatoes all the way around the center with a knife.\n3. Place your potatoes in the pot and boil for about 15 minutes or so.\n4. Prepare a bowl of ice water and place your potatoes in the water.\n5. Swish it around for a few seconds just enough so that it’s cool enough to handle.\n6. Remove your potato from the water and remove the skin.\n\nThe ice water bath helps to stop the cooking process and cool down the potatoes quickly, making the skin easier to peel off.", "question": "how to boil potatoes for easy peeling", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.505, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1233, 321, 3080, 27582, 11, 1221, 8317, 311, 458, 9853, 3015, 8885, 13, 3197, 7010, 11, 57112, 1007, 6787, 13, 320, 16, 20, 4420, 311, 4296, 11, 220, 20, 6486, 311, 57112, 1817, 29105, 38734, 8], "logp_list": [-1.0567, -0.0124, -0.7014, -0.0123, -0.1246, -0.017, -0.0028, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0009, -0.0018, -0.0022, -0.0239, -0.0744, -0.0034, -0.0018, -0.0011, -0.0046, -0.0036, -0.0532, -1.1633, -0.0021, -0.0002, -0.0018, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0028, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0024, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0005, -0.0538], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3304, "norm_logp": -0.0925, "input_len": 364, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.37, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "337", "pred": "Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick. Pull on the tick gently and steadily.", "raw_output": "Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick. Pull on the tick gently and steadily.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to get rid of ticks from dogs:\n\n1. Check your dog's body thoroughly, especially in areas like inside and behind the ears, around the eyes, and other favorite tick hiding places.\n2. Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin, and wear gloves if needed.\n3. Pull on the tick gently and steadily, making sure not to yank it away from your dog too quickly.\n4. Regularly use tick control products on your dog, as recommended by your veterinarian, to prevent ticks from attaching to your dog in the first place.\n\nIf you have any further questions or concerns, consult your veterinarian for advice.", "question": "how to get rid of ticks from dogs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.597, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 43, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [10253, 264, 6716, 315, 14545, 10125, 388, 311, 33377, 279, 1968, 315, 279, 9341, 1380, 432, 84503, 311, 279, 6787, 13, 41062, 35416, 421, 498, 3119, 311, 990, 697, 19225, 311, 4057, 279, 9341, 13, 31828, 389, 279, 9341, 29273, 323, 41971, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3101, -0.0523, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0176, -0.0068, -0.0013, -0.0053, -0.0275, -0.0101, -0.0025, -0.0302, -0.0031, -0.0102, -0.0075, -0.0046, -0.0018, -0.1352, -0.2894, -0.0031, -0.1318, -0.0044, -0.0027, -0.0025, -0.0062, -0.0068, -0.0031, -0.0013, -0.0029, -0.0029, -0.0016, -0.0566, -0.0466, -0.002, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0019, -0.0014, -0.0015, -0.0493], "cumulative_logprob": -2.2466, "norm_logp": -0.0522, "input_len": 479, "output_tokens": 43, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.19, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "341", "pred": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1.Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\n2.Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side down on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook another", "raw_output": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1.Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\n2.Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side down on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook another", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can make ribs:\n\nStep 1: Prepare the ribs\n\n* Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs (passage 3)\n* Season the ribs with a dry rub and mustard mixture (passage 2)\n\nStep 2: Grill the ribs\n\n* Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium heat (passage 1)\n* Place the ribs meaty-side up on the grill and brush with some of the sauce (passage 1)\n* Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes, then flip and brush the ribs with more sauce (passage 1)\n* Continue to cook and flip every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through (passage 1)\n\nTotal grilling time: 20 minutes\n\nNote: The passages do not mention the temperature at which to cook the ribs, but a medium heat of 350°F to 450°F is suggested.", "question": "how to make ribs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.28, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 76, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1281, 55149, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 51325, 266, 264, 6819, 476, 60103, 39058, 311, 11051, 320, 18, 20, 15, 58472, 311, 220, 19, 20, 15, 58472, 4292, 17, 13270, 279, 55149, 504, 279, 46399, 11, 1992, 1105, 13041, 88, 24067, 1495, 389, 279, 39058, 11, 323, 15625, 1105, 448, 1045, 315, 279, 19187, 13, 17757, 279, 39058, 323, 4296, 369, 220, 20, 4420, 13, 40284, 323, 15625, 279, 55149, 323, 4296, 2441, 220], "logp_list": [-0.4448, -0.2785, -0.0372, -0.0689, -1.5047, -0.2215, -0.0225, -0.6045, -0.2059, -1.2987, -0.0004, -0.0634, -0.0166, -0.0027, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0019, -0.0045, -0.1137, -0.1593, -0.0118, -0.0001, -0.0082, -0.0033, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.9247, -0.0085, -0.1845, -0.0038, -0.0095, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0344, -0.0019, -0.0772, -0.0054, -0.0001, -0.0024, -0.2992, -0.0068, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.0087, -0.0008, -0.0009, -0.0085, -0.0011, -0.0012, -0.0016, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.5337, -0.052, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0012, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.3183, -0.0157, -0.0041, -0.0005, -0.0031, -0.0002, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0002], "cumulative_logprob": -7.6009, "norm_logp": -0.1, "input_len": 360, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "345", "pred": "No", "raw_output": "No", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the answer to the question \"When you have a bowel movement, do you lose weight?\" is: No, having a bowel movement does not result in a significant loss of body weight or body fat. The weight loss associated with having a bowel movement is minimal and temporary, and it is not a reliable indicator of weight loss or fat loss. Instead, weight loss occurs through a calorie deficit, which forces the body to burn stored fat for energy.", "question": "when you have a bowel movement do you lose weight", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 1.114, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 1, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [2753], "logp_list": [-0.4631], "cumulative_logprob": -0.4631, "norm_logp": -0.4631, "input_len": 422, "output_tokens": 1, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.64, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "354", "pred": "Company goodwill is the reputation and connection with customers that tend to make that connection permanent. It represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation possesses. Goodwill is important in business because it will increase your customer base and retain old clients, attract investors and attract future buyers.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill is the reputation and connection with customers that tend to make that connection permanent. It represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation possesses. Goodwill is important in business because it will increase your customer base and retain old clients, attract investors and attract future buyers.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, I can confidently answer the following questions:\n\nWhat is company goodwill?\nCompany goodwill refers to the positive image and reputation of a business that is built over time through providing quality products or services, satisfying customers, and building strong relationships with them. Goodwill is a valuable asset for any business as it helps to attract new customers, retain existing ones, and attract investors.\n\nWhy is goodwill important in business?\nGoodwill is important in business because it helps to build trust and loyalty among customers, which leads to long-term relationships and repeat business. A strong reputation and goodwill can also make a business more attractive to potential investors and buyers. Additionally, goodwill can help a business to stand out from its competitors and establish a unique identity in the marketplace.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.497, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 59, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [14491, 81646, 374, 279, 17011, 323, 3633, 448, 6310, 429, 8376, 311, 1281, 429, 3633, 15330, 13, 1084, 10868, 279, 897, 315, 279, 32364, 311, 279, 6310, 892, 279, 829, 323, 17011, 49226, 13, 7684, 14387, 374, 2989, 304, 2562, 1576, 432, 686, 5263, 697, 6002, 2331, 323, 14060, 2310, 8239, 11, 9320, 14823, 323, 9320, 3853, 22630, 13], "logp_list": [-0.5691, -0.0343, -0.4646, -0.2769, -0.4717, -0.0945, -0.13, -0.1622, -0.0069, -0.8529, -1.1105, -0.0001, -0.0116, -0.763, -0.0172, -0.0033, -0.1704, -0.1137, -0.4629, -0.0422, -0.1507, -0.002, -0.0475, -0.0357, -0.0132, -0.1131, -0.0083, -0.1794, -0.0022, -0.0413, -0.0048, -0.0004, -0.0801, -0.0622, -1.0711, -0.007, -0.0354, -0.0176, -0.3653, -0.0016, -0.0987, -0.0035, -0.4551, -0.0094, -0.1936, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0796, -0.001, -0.001, -0.0019, -0.0101, -0.0188, -0.0004, -0.03, -0.0024, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0168], "cumulative_logprob": -8.9201, "norm_logp": -0.1512, "input_len": 294, "output_tokens": 59, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.79, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "359", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an ad hominem fallacy is an attack on an argument made by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered and redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties. In summary, both types of attacks involve attacking the person making the argument, but poisoning the well fallacy specifically takes place before the merits of a case are considered.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.022, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 67, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13], "logp_list": [-0.78, -0.4245, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0056, -0.0001, -0.3476, -0.1305, -0.1243, -0.2136, -0.1607, -0.0166, -0.0015, -0.0006, -0.9163, -0.0001, -0.07, -0.0018, -0.0101, -0.1138, -0.0001, -0.3572, -0.2596, -0.0001, -0.0875, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0437, -0.1701, -0.4708, -0.2041, -0.0025, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0, -1.142, -0.0337, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0102, -0.5242, -0.2486, -0.1036, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0083, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.8911, -0.0592, -0.0171, -0.0083, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0485], "cumulative_logprob": -8.0204, "norm_logp": -0.1197, "input_len": 244, "output_tokens": 67, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 70.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "367", "pred": "Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are from rocks. Rock can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand. On the other hand, stone is just a small piece or pebble that can be carried", "raw_output": "Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are from rocks. Rock can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand. On the other hand, stone is just a small piece or pebble that can be carried", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust that is normally exposed on the surface or underlying the soil. On the other hand, a stone is described as a smaller piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. The passages also mention that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, while rocks cannot. Therefore, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size, with rocks being larger and stones being smaller and easier to handle.", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.694, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 51, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [49, 25183, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 323, 26210, 525, 504, 23035, 13, 9127, 646, 387, 7481, 438, 264, 3460, 6573, 315, 9798, 429, 374, 5000, 311, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 13, 1913, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 9798, 374, 1101, 264, 2613, 6573, 476, 1051, 59880, 429, 646, 387, 11691], "logp_list": [-1.3765, -0.0009, -0.2863, -0.3606, -0.0035, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.034, -0.0009, -0.0066, -0.071, -0.0091, -0.0265, -0.8703, -0.0593, -0.005, -0.0056, -0.0038, -0.0026, -0.0047, -0.0011, -0.0021, -0.0008, -0.0141, -0.0022, -0.0031, -0.0004, -0.0045, -0.0016, -0.004, -0.0057, -0.0005, -0.0492, -0.0231, -0.0008, -0.0061, -0.0156, -0.0029, -0.0383, -0.0014, -0.0045, -0.0012, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.0034, -0.0003, -0.0017, -0.0158, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0023], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3383, "norm_logp": -0.0655, "input_len": 495, "output_tokens": 51, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.28, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "381", "pred": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. The cells in your worksheet highlight to confirm they're selected.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option.\n3. Click on", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. The cells in your worksheet highlight to confirm they're selected.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option.\n3. Click on", "gt": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, first click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. Then, switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option. Click on the Sort By drop-down menu to select a column by name. The names correspond to the headings typed in the first row of your data. Alternatively, you can use Kutools for Excel's Sort Sheets tool to sort all worksheets.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 14.805, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 72, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 30893, 458, 21029, 10834, 27790, 2673, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 9189, 389, 279, 8427, 2009, 3137, 518, 279, 19246, 315, 697, 2802, 323, 3250, 69452, 13, 576, 7761, 304, 697, 36636, 11167, 311, 7683, 807, 2299, 4091, 624, 17, 13, 15586, 311, 279, 2885, 5651, 304, 279, 5100, 21029, 44567, 323, 24523, 279, 15967, 609, 12339, 1874, 13, 9189, 389, 279, 15967, 2999, 624, 18, 13, 9189, 389], "logp_list": [-0.5105, -0.0461, -0.0081, -0.0891, -0.006