{"id": "427", "pred": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other", "raw_output": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other", "gt": "Based on the given passages, I am unable to answer the question \"What is a UPC description?\" as none of the passages provide a clear definition or explanation of what a UPC description is.", "question": "what is a upc description", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.406, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 25500, 5643, 6119, 320, 3124, 34, 8, 374, 264, 49407, 66654, 2449, 429, 374, 13570, 1483, 304, 279, 3639, 4180, 11, 6864, 11, 3639, 15072, 11, 8330, 11, 1532, 16911, 11, 304, 4505, 323, 1008], "logp_list": [-1.153, -0.4727, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0725, -0.002, -0.0024, -0.021, -0.0555, -0.0096, -0.0029, -0.0032, -0.0, -1.1307, -0.0105, -0.0075, -0.0003, -0.0625, -0.0587, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0049, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0097, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0134, -0.0003, -0.0195, -0.0898], "cumulative_logprob": -3.2154, "norm_logp": -0.0893, "input_len": 390, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.56, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "431", "pred": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "raw_output": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first determine the extent of the tarnish. If the jewelry is slightly dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth into the solution. Rub the jewelry with the cloth, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the jewelry and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. If your silver jewelry is lightly tarnished, you can also try submerging it in a hot saltwater bath. Ensure that if your jewelry has gemstones, they won't be affected by the saltwater. For diamonds, clean with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, and pat dry with a soft cloth.", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.988, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 76, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [58083, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 568], "logp_list": [-2.2127, -0.167, -0.1472, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0053, -0.0018, -0.0091, -0.0031, -0.0002, -0.2067, -0.0209, -0.1891, -0.0017, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.2367, -0.0468, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0372, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0041, -0.0041, -0.0004, -0.0044, -0.0019, -0.0016, -0.0015, -0.0013, -0.0095, -0.0006, -0.0227, -0.5563, -0.0379, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0037, -0.0118, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0065, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0023, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0622, -0.0155, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0029, -0.0009, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.7873, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0002, -0.0012, -0.0594], "cumulative_logprob": -4.9278, "norm_logp": -0.0648, "input_len": 349, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "435", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "raw_output": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "gt": "To find someone in France, you can use a website for people search in France. Sending an email or letter to the Town Hall in the person's city of birth can also be helpful, especially if you have lost relatives or friends. Try to find out the person's middle name, it could make the search easier. You can also use the French white pages, available in English at www.pagesjaunes.fr. Enter the person's last name in the 'Nom' field and first name in the 'Prenom' field, enter address information if known, and then click 'Trouver' to search. For searching in Paris and its suburbs, instead of typing Paris in the City field, write IDF (or Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field. Posting a missed connection on Paris Craigslist might also be helpful.", "question": "how to find someone in france", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.966, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 49, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1477, 4325, 304, 9625, 11, 498, 646, 3947, 8438, 37980, 5580, 8531, 18798, 11, 279, 21772, 2502, 82181, 11, 892, 374, 279, 8585, 2319, 315, 279, 4158, 6816, 13, 11252, 279, 1697, 594, 1537, 829, 304, 279, 36900, 2070, 13, 1446, 646, 3725, 264, 1156, 829, 304, 393], "logp_list": [-0.3293, -0.0043, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0229, -0.223, -0.0252, -1.6119, -0.7169, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.1457, -0.1807, -0.7629, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0865, -0.1373, -0.005, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.0018, -0.0098, -0.0003, -0.0076, -0.0154, -0.0039, -0.4859, -0.0397, -0.1079, -0.0524, -0.1201, -0.0006, -0.0824, -0.0028, -0.5882, -0.2827, -0.0061, -0.2117, -0.0209, -0.0018, -0.0018, -0.0186, -0.3196], "cumulative_logprob": -6.645, "norm_logp": -0.1356, "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 49, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.19, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "440", "pred": "Fire is the mechanism by which the forest is continually regenerated, states Hall. Fires consume dead, decaying vegetation accumulating on the forest floor, thereby clearing the way for new growth. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonon (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out", "raw_output": "Fire is the mechanism by which the forest is continually regenerated, states Hall. Fires consume dead, decaying vegetation accumulating on the forest floor, thereby clearing the way for new growth. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonon (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out", "gt": "Forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines because they help in the regeneration of the forest. Fires consume dead and decaying vegetation, clearing the way for new growth. Jack pines rely on fire to spread their seeds. They produce seratonous (resin-filled) cones that remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin, causing the cones to open and the seeds to fall or blow out. Furthermore, fires can disrupt the growth of other trees like spruce, providing an advantage to the more fire-tolerant jack pines and allowing them to proliferate.", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 19.423, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 96, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16697, 374, 279, 16953, 553, 892, 279, 13638, 374, 34511, 70353, 11, 5302, 10926, 13, 82830, 24057, 5593, 11, 1622, 17270,