{"id": "106", "pred": "The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive and the nervous systems work together to help keep the body functioning normally.", "raw_output": "The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive and the nervous systems work together to help keep the body functioning normally.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the body systems work together as follows:\n\nThe skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, and reproductive systems all work together to help keep the body functioning normally. The urinary system helps to clean waste products from the other systems, while the respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the other systems. The nervous system plays a crucial role in coordinating the functions of all the other systems.", "question": "how the body systems work together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.333, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 37, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 68297, 11, 51092, 11, 58971, 11, 41531, 11, 4225, 37120, 11, 71635, 11, 835, 77638, 11, 42645, 774, 11, 22077, 11, 41789, 323, 279, 22596, 5942, 975, 3786, 311, 1492, 2506, 279, 2487, 30201, 14279, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4445, -2.6672, -0.1113, -0.002, -0.0011, -0.0037, -0.0007, -0.0037, -0.0013, -0.0055, -0.0001, -0.0228, -0.0309, -0.0053, -0.0075, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0074, -0.0, -0.0016, -0.0035, -0.0112, -0.1429, -0.402, -0.3893, -0.0004, -0.2223, -0.425, -0.1125, -0.1426, -0.6006, -0.0319, -0.0073, -0.3879, -0.0008, -0.0053, -0.0427], "cumulative_logprob": -6.2459, "norm_logp": -0.1688, "input_len": 242, "output_tokens": 37, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.72, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "111", "pred": "The Leeward Islands are the northwestern islands of the Lesser Antilles. They’re called the Leeward Islands because they’re away from the wind (“lee”).", "raw_output": "The Leeward Islands are the northwestern islands of the Lesser Antilles. They’re called the Leeward Islands because they’re away from the wind (“lee”).", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main difference between the Leeward and Windward Islands is their exposure to the wind and precipitation. The Windward Islands are exposed to the northeasterly trade winds and receive more rainfall, making them wetter and more humid, while the Leeward Islands are located on the leeward side of the mountains and receive less rainfall, making them drier. Therefore, the answer to the question is:\n\nThe Windward Islands are wetter and more humid due to their exposure to the northeasterly trade winds, while the Leeward Islands are drier and receive less rainfall.", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.024, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 32, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 1967, 24035, 22235, 525, 279, 10200, 30282, 29000, 315, 279, 13907, 6755, 30255, 13, 2379, 3129, 2598, 279, 1967, 24035, 22235, 1576, 807, 3129, 3123, 504, 279, 9956, 26087, 8501, 64212], "logp_list": [-0.1224, -1.8214, -0.0002, -0.0688, -0.2357, -0.0877, -0.1187, -0.0015, -0.0018, -0.0195, -0.0018, -0.0052, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.2047, -0.4356, -0.1248, -0.0043, -0.0016, -0.0067, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0037, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0066, -0.0576, -0.0066, -0.1678], "cumulative_logprob": -3.5094, "norm_logp": -0.1097, "input_len": 285, "output_tokens": 32, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 16.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 3.3, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "115", "pred": "A fee of $75.00 must be paid to process a Water Stock Certificate transfer.", "raw_output": "A fee of $75.00 must be paid to process a Water Stock Certificate transfer.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, water shares can be transferred through the issuance of a new Water Stock Certificate to the new owner of record after paying a fee of $75.00. The seller must return the Water Stock Certificate assigned to them and sign the back of the certificate. The new owner must provide proof of ownership before a transfer can be processed. Additionally, an additional fee of $75.00 must be paid when filing a Lost Certificate Affidavit form, which is furnished by the company and must be signed and notarized.", "question": "how are water shares transferred", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.239, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 20, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 11060, 315, 400, 22, 20, 13, 15, 15, 1969, 387, 7171, 311, 1882, 264, 9959, 12648, 31402, 8317, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3381, -0.3324, -0.0026, -0.014, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.009, -0.0021, -0.0002, -0.0095, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0407, -0.0184, -0.0026, -0.0124, -0.0005, -0.0029, -0.0054, -0.1015], "cumulative_logprob": -1.894, "norm_logp": -0.0947, "input_len": 333, "output_tokens": 20, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.35, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "119", "pred": "The name Teresa is a Greek baby name. In Greek, the meaning of the name Teresa is: Reap; from Therasia.", "raw_output": "The name Teresa is a Greek baby name. In Greek, the meaning of the name Teresa is: Reap; from Therasia.", "gt": "The name Teresa is of Greek, American, Italian, and Spanish origin. In Greek and American, it means 'Reap; from Therasia.' In Italian, it is interpreted as 'Harvester.' In Spanish, the name is associated with two popular saints, 'Teresa of Avila' and 'Therese of Lisieux.' The name is also thought to originate from the Greek “therizein” meaning “to harvest, harvester” or from the Greek “theros” meaning “summer”.", "question": "teresa name meaning", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 5.668, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 28, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 829, 63440, 374, 264, 17860, 8770, 829, 13, 758, 17860, 11, 279, 7290, 315, 279, 829, 63440, 374, 25, 1032, 391, 26, 504, 663, 9247, 685, 13], "logp_list": [-0.7573, -0.2003, -0.0535, -1.1978, -0.2488, -0.0222, -0.1235, -0.0002, -0.8159, -0.0703, -0.0033, -0.3173, -0.1427, -0.0191, -0.006, -0.024, -0.0001, -0.0136, -0.0021, -0.6642, -0.0979, -0.0006, -0.0139, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0334], "cumulative_logprob": -4.8288, "norm_logp": -0.1725, "input_len": 504, "output_tokens": 28, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.74, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "125", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, first prepare the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel. Cut the two 4 x 4 skids at 7/4″. Set and level the skids following the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7/4″ and six joists at 44 1/4″. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as shown in the floor framing section of the lean to shed plans. Check for square, and then anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.", "raw_output": "To build a lean-to shed, first prepare the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel. Cut the two 4 x 4 skids at 7/4″. Set and level the skids following the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7/4″ and six joists at 44 1/4″. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as shown in the floor framing section of the lean to shed plans. Check for square, and then anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the process of building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, cutting and setting the 4 x 4 skids, cutting and assembling the floor with 2 x 6 rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to the skids, attaching tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, constructing the walls separately, and raising them onto the frame.", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 24.931, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 126, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1936, 264, 15651, 4686, 24354, 11, 1156, 10549, 279, 2747, 448, 264, 220, 19, 21580, 6193, 315, 16830, 291, 41523, 13, 19649, 279, 1378, 220, 19, 856, 220, 19, 1901, 3365, 518, 220, 22, 14, 19, 21580, 13, 2573, 323, 2188, 279, 1901, 3365, 2701, 279, 24354, 4752, 6649, 13, 19649, 1378, 220, 17, 856, 220, 21, 30130, 7647, 1671, 518, 220, 22, 14, 19, 21580, 323, 4743, 7647, 1671, 518, 220, 19, 19, 220, 16, 14, 19, 21580, 13, 1634, 15790, 279, 6422, 323, 738, 432, 389, 279, 1901, 3365, 438, 6839, 304, 279, 6422, 57949, 3772, 315, 279, 15651, 311, 24354, 6649, 13, 4248, 369, 9334, 11, 323, 1221, 17105, 279, 4034, 311, 279, 1901, 3365, 448, 3040, 7647, 380, 12327, 25941, 13], "logp_list": [-1.5985, -0.1632, -0.0089, -0.022, -0.0059, -0.0002, -0.0622, -1.5813, -0.1334, -0.0061, -0.0091, -0.082, -0.0119, -0.0149, -0.0016, -0.6064, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.2305, -0.3522, -0.2629, -0.0357, -0.0158, -0.0018, -0.0225, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0393, -0.017, -0.0049, -0.4367, -0.0346, -0.4203, -0.8823, -0.0119, -0.004, -0.0002, -0.0046, -0.0007, -0.0002, -1.1616, -0.0026, -0.0256, -0.0054, -0.0034, -0.0145, -0.0241, -0.0151, -0.003, -0.0036, -0.0034, -0.0002, -0.0009, -0.0005, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.0125, -0.0039, -0.1915, -0.0038, -0.0059, -0.3491, -0.0313, -0.0058, -0.0016, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.1577, -0.0132, -0.0036, -0.8061, -0.0017, -0.0744, -0.0298, -0.0055, -0.0423, -0.0002, -0.0009, -0.0104, -0.012, -0.0075, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.001, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.1018, -0.0163, -0.0025, -0.001, -0.0084, -0.0022, -0.002, -0.0048, -0.0014, -0.1272, -0.3575, -0.0009, -0.0021, -0.007, -0.0111, -0.0009, -0.0007, -0.0711, -0.0237, -0.0007, -0.0382, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.001, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0079, -0.0144, -0.0034, -0.0006, -0.0114, -0.0012, -0.0522], "cumulative_logprob": -11.0052, "norm_logp": -0.0873, "input_len": 371, "output_tokens": 126, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.18, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "130", "pred": "India supported Vietnam's independence from France, opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, and supported the unification of Vietnam.", "raw_output": "India supported Vietnam's independence from France, opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, and supported the unification of Vietnam.", "gt": "The passages suggest that trends in India are more typical than those in Vietnam, particularly in the area of education. Vietnam's education system is described as relatively equitable, where poorer children receive the same treatment as their better-off counterparts. In contrast, India's early focus was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, and training teachers. Politically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. Both countries have a history of colonialism, with India being a former colony of the United Kingdom and Vietnam of France. Post-colonization, India experienced division and conflict among Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs. Cultural and economic links between India and Vietnam date back to the 2nd century.", "question": "difference between vietnam and india", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 4.953, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 24, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [33548, 7248, 22500, 594, 23665, 504, 9625, 11, 16087, 3693, 21587, 304, 279, 22500, 5004, 11, 323, 7248, 279, 650, 2404, 315, 22500, 13], "logp_list": [-1.2275, -0.4954, -0.0165, -0.1137, -0.0026, -0.0629, -0.0067, -0.3055, -0.0386, -0.0379, -0.0015, -0.0067, -0.0029, -0.0026, -0.01, -0.0132, -0.0128, -0.0022, -0.8839, -0.5968, -0.0031, -0.0135, -0.0023, -0.1986], "cumulative_logprob": -4.0574, "norm_logp": -0.1691, "input_len": 415, "output_tokens": 24, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "136", "pred": "Preheat your oven to 400°F (205° C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.) Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil on each exposed head, using your", "raw_output": "Preheat your oven to 400°F (205° C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.) Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil on each exposed head, using your", "gt": "Sure! Here's how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nTo roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of each clove, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices, if desired.\n7. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for up to 45 minutes or until the cloves are softened to your liking.\n8. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nNote that the roasting time may vary depending on the size of your garlic cloves, so check on them periodically to avoid overcooking.", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 23.684, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 120, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4703, 19963, 697, 23387, 311, 220, 19, 15, 15, 58472, 320, 17, 15, 20, 11616, 356, 568, 88594, 323, 37867, 279, 25298, 722, 15955, 13617, 315, 279, 4361, 30635, 45812, 11, 9380, 34439, 279, 49950, 315, 279, 3842, 84288, 315, 30635, 13, 12091, 264, 17232, 21430, 11, 3931, 220, 16, 14, 19, 311, 264, 220, 16, 14, 17, 17122, 504, 279, 1909, 315, 84288, 11, 45966, 279, 3842, 84288, 315, 30635, 13, 10771, 279, 30635, 14629, 304, 264, 27815, 7215, 11, 3931, 3108, 705, 13, 320, 32, 54304, 258, 7215, 4278, 2244, 369, 419, 11, 438, 432, 13598, 279, 30635, 53220, 504, 20097, 2163, 6138, 2926, 43137, 264, 5625, 92100, 315, 32113, 5590, 389, 1817, 14900, 1968, 11, 1667, 697], "logp_list": [-1.0049, -0.0014, -0.2591, -0.0003, -0.0042, -0.001, -0.0024, -0.0012, -0.0001, -0.0258, -0.0566, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.021, -0.2858, -0.1746, -0.4182, -0.0032, -0.0023, -0.0003, -0.0028, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0049, -0.0111, -0.0075, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0113, -0.005, -0.0055, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0015, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0133, -0.0001, -0.003, -0.0411, -0.0002, -0.0014, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0007, -0.0036, -0.0023, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0019, -0.1138, -0.0014, -0.0026, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0024, -0.0109, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0016, -0.0002, -0.0084, -0.0247, -0.0015, -0.0009, -0.0133, -0.0039, -0.0003, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0052, -0.003, -0.0037, -0.0994, -0.0784, -0.7134, -0.0023, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0011, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.049, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0089, -0.0137, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0386, -0.0019, -0.0437, -0.0072, -0.0131, -0.0004, -0.0003], "cumulative_logprob": -3.6648, "norm_logp": -0.0305, "input_len": 371, "output_tokens": 120, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.85, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "140", "pred": "When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 44(6) of the Income Tax Act, 1961 permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 44(6) of the Income Tax Act, 1961 permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, the Income Tax Act allows the scrap value of the asset to be deducted from the opening work-in-progress (WDV) of such asset in the previous year in which it was discarded. This reduction is taken into account while computing the depreciation expense for the subsequent years. However, any changes made to the value of the block must be made strictly in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of Income Tax Act 1961.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.552, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 48, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4498, 458, 9329, 374, 43210, 553, 279, 2562, 714, 537, 6088, 11, 3772, 220, 19, 19, 7, 21, 8, 315, 279, 32520, 15190, 3218, 11, 220, 16, 24, 21, 16, 28690, 279, 20843, 897, 315, 279, 9329, 311, 387, 11046, 504, 279, 2504, 304, 279, 3681, 1042, 304], "logp_list": [-1.4536, -0.0066, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0013, -0.8514, -0.3528, -0.0013, -0.0887, -0.0115, -0.0001, -0.0099, -1.1302, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.2955, -0.0251, -0.019, -0.0317, -0.1399, -0.0029, -0.007, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.3989, -0.0024, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.2478, -0.0138, -0.005, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0057, -0.0014, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0311, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0038, -0.0037, -0.0018, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0251], "cumulative_logprob": -5.1752, "norm_logp": -0.1078, "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 48, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.75, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "146", "pred": "Trace the pattern to the rest of the fabric and cut. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper (1/4″ away from the center) with right side facing each other. Change the presserr foot to zipper foot, sew a line near to the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of them with right side facing down. Stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of main zipper gusset, top stitch near the seam line. Repeat sewing another side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper in between the gusset should measure 1/2″. Change the press foot back to normal foot, stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset so that the main and lining fabric attach together nicely.", "raw_output": "Trace the pattern to the rest of the fabric and cut. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper (1/4″ away from the center) with right side facing each other. Change the presserr foot to zipper foot, sew a line near to the seam line to secure the position. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of them with right side facing down. Stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of main zipper gusset, top stitch near the seam line. Repeat sewing another side of the zipper gusset. The width of the zipper in between the gusset should measure 1/2″. Change the press foot back to normal foot, stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset so that the main and lining fabric attach together nicely.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to sew a zipper gusset:\n\n1. Make a casing in the side seams by stitching the outer edge of the gusset and the back side seam.\n2. Slide the boning right down into the casing.\n3. Push the seam allowance to the back and tack the casing to the lining, if necessary, so it doesn't move.\n4. Make a zipper sandwich by placing one piece of lining fabric face up, followed by the zipper, and then the outer fabric right side facing down.\n5. Sew across the top of the zipper sandwich with a zipper foot, making sure to line up the three edges.\n6. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper.\n7. Trace all pattern pieces to the remaining fabric and cut them out.\n8. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, with the right sides facing each other, and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position.\n9. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of the zipper gusset, with the right sides facing down, and stitch along the seam line.\n10. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line.\n11. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset.\n12. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2\".\n13. Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch along the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabrics together nicely.\n\nTherefore, to sew a zipper gusset, you need to make a casing in the side seams, insert the boning, make a zipper sandwich, sew the zipper gusset to the zipper, and attach the lining to the zipper gusset.", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 44.113, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 177, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [6550, 279, 5383, 311, 279, 2732, 315, 279, 13055, 323, 3931, 13, 7405, 264, 2421, 61936, 3143, 279, 1909, 2936, 315, 279, 68761, 13, 17471, 279, 68761, 342, 355, 746, 311, 279, 68761, 320, 16, 14, 19, 21580, 3123, 504, 279, 4126, 8, 448, 1290, 3108, 12880, 1817, 1008, 13, 10388, 279, 3493, 261, 81, 4478, 311, 68761, 4478, 11, 22352, 264, 1555, 3143, 311, 279, 26396, 1555, 311, 9767, 279, 2309, 13, 10771, 279, 68761, 342, 355, 746, 35371, 389, 1909, 315, 1105, 448, 1290, 3108, 12880, 1495, 13, 67923, 389, 279, 26396, 1555, 13, 40284, 279, 35371