{"id": "231", "pred": "There are several methods for cleaning these stains, though the size of the oil spill and length of time the stain has had to set will determine the process for removal.", "raw_output": "There are several methods for cleaning these stains, though the size of the oil spill and length of time the stain has had to set will determine the process for removal.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question \"How do you remove oil stains from a concrete driveway?\"\n\nTo remove oil stains from a concrete driveway, you can use one of the following methods:\n\n1. Oven cleaner, goop hand cleaner, and carburetor cleaner (as mentioned in Passage 1).\n2. A commercial-grade cleaner applied with a brush or broom, followed by scrubbing with a pressure washer (as described in Passage 3).\n\nBefore starting the cleaning process, it is recommended to wash away any dirt or debris from the affected area (Passage 2). Additionally, it is important to avoid using high-pressure hoses to clean the stain, as this can push the oil deeper into the pavement (Passage 2).\n\nIt is also noted that the size of the oil spill and the length of time the stain has been present will affect the method of removal (Passage 3). For small and stubborn stains, the poultice method may be effective (Passage 2).", "question": "oil stain on concrete driveway", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 6.488, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 33, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [3862, 525, 3807, 5413, 369, 15826, 1493, 62463, 11, 3498, 279, 1379, 315, 279, 5590, 38797, 323, 3084, 315, 882, 279, 51964, 702, 1030, 311, 738, 686, 8253, 279, 1882, 369, 16652, 13], "logp_list": [-1.6817, -0.0508, -0.5437, -0.0242, -0.0422, -0.0069, -0.3199, -0.0089, -0.1726, -0.0548, -0.0061, -0.0029, -0.0018, -0.0012, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0021, -0.0031, -0.0009, -0.002, -0.0049, -0.0016, -0.6064, -0.0024, -0.0207, -0.0002, -0.0018, -0.0008, -0.0008, -0.0005, -0.0017, -0.0034, -0.0305], "cumulative_logprob": -3.6024, "norm_logp": -0.1092, "input_len": 421, "output_tokens": 33, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.47, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "240", "pred": "Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps or pots are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out.", "raw_output": "Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps or pots are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a crab trap is a large square metal cage with a system of nets inside that allows crabs to enter through a narrow slit at the top. To set the trap, the ends with lead-ins are placed in the water and the crabs are attracted to the free meal offered by the trap. The Alaska Department of Fish and Game recommends using king crab pots that range from 6 by 6 feet to 8 by 8 feet with heights between 2 and 3 feet. Over time, these mechanisms may disable the trap, preventing it from ghost fishing.", "question": "how do crab traps work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.445, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 46, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16001, 370, 44560, 975, 553, 1667, 264, 1849, 315, 52457, 4766, 264, 3460, 9317, 35360, 1865, 315, 9067, 476, 10063, 9317, 11294, 429, 374, 48580, 448, 9509, 15904, 13, 576, 44560, 476, 50031, 525, 6188, 311, 2138, 1560, 3435, 304, 714, 537, 1077, 1105, 700, 13], "logp_list": [-0.3833, -0.0005, -0.0615, -0.3189, -0.0738, -0.1634, -0.0418, -0.035, -0.0019, -0.0011, -0.01, -0.0066, -0.0069, -0.0058, -0.0003, -0.1865, -0.004, -0.0012, -0.0057, -0.0203, -0.0027, -0.0007, -0.0651, -0.0771, -0.0025, -0.0007, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0444, -0.2319, -0.2897, -0.5523, -0.0004, -0.0163, -0.0085, -0.0006, -0.0016, -0.0902, -0.002, -0.0025, -0.0087, -0.0016, -0.4367, -0.023, -0.0057, -0.1], "cumulative_logprob": -3.2952, "norm_logp"