{"id": "324", "pred": "Based on the information provided in the text, homemade texture or decorative paint rollers can work better than the commercial variety because many faux finishes rely on applying a random pattern of texture to your walls, and commercial texture", "raw_output": "Based on the information provided in the text, homemade texture or decorative paint rollers can work better than the commercial variety because many faux finishes rely on applying a random pattern of texture to your walls, and commercial texture", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's what we can conclude about how well patterned rollers work:\n\nPassage 1 suggests that homemade texture or decorative paint rollers can work better than commercial ones for certain faux finishes because they can create a random pattern of texture.\n\nPassage 2 provides a personal testimonial about the effectiveness of the Dermaroller for improving the appearance of skin. The writer claims that it makes their skin look younger and healthier, and that it works best when used with serums or moisturizers. However, there is no direct mention of patterned rollers in this passage.\n\nPassage 3 provides instructions for creating a basic texture roller using an old roller pad with clumped tufts and random patterns created by stretching and twisting rubber bands. This passage suggests that patterned rollers can create a textured finish on walls, but there is no information on how effective they are or how well they work.\n\nTherefore, based on the given passages, it is difficult to say definitively how well patterned rollers work. Passage 1 suggests that they may be effective for certain applications, while passage 2 provides a personal endorsement of a specific product (Dermaroller) for improving skin appearance. Passage 3 provides instructions for creating a basic texture roller, but does not provide any information on its effectiveness. Therefore, the answer to the original question is: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how well do patterned rollers work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 8.054, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 41, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 1995, 3897, 304, 279, 1467, 11, 35333, 10434, 476, 45436, 6177, 88613, 646, 975, 2664, 1091, 279, 8353, 8045, 1576, 1657, 50427, 33036, 17188, 389, 18950, 264, 4194, 5383, 315, 10434, 311, 697, 14285, 11, 323, 8353, 10434], "logp_list": [-1.9472, -0.0004, -0.0056, -0.5061, -0.0329, -0.4875, -0.0227, -0.7808, -0.0281, -1.582, -0.1482, -0.0251, -0.0026, -0.0046, -0.0006, -0.5596, -0.125, -0.4863, -0.0232, -0.4297, -0.0002, -0.0035, -1.0466, -0.5831, -0.0004, -0.0015, -0.0041, -0.0, -0.0021, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0047, -0.0001, -0.0131, -0.1078, -0.0, -0.0787, -0.0305, -0.0077, -0.0002], "cumulative_logprob": -9.0841, "norm_logp": -0.2216, "input_len": 382, "output_tokens": 41, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "332", "pred": "Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick. Pull on the tick gently and steadily.", "raw_output": "Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick. Pull on the tick gently and steadily.", "gt": "To get rid of ticks from dogs, first, check common tick hiding places such as inside and behind the dog's ears and around the eyes. Once you locate a tick, use a pair of tweezers to grasp the tick's head where it attaches to the skin. If you plan to use your fingers, wear gloves. Pull on the tick gently and steadily to avoid leaving part of the tick's mouth behind, which can cause an infection. Regular use of tick control products can also prevent ticks from attaching to your dog. Your veterinarian can advise you about the best product for your dog and your situation. Flea and tick shampoos can kill the ticks that are already on your pet.", "question": "how to get rid of ticks from dogs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.37, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 43, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [10253, 264, 6716, 315, 14545, 10125, 388, 311, 33377, 279, 1968, 315, 279, 9341, 1380, 432, 84503, 311, 279, 6787, 13, 41062, 35416, 421, 498, 3119, 311, 990, 697, 19225, 311, 4057, 279, 9341, 13, 31828, 389, 279, 9341, 29273, 323, 41971, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3101, -0.0523, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0176, -0.0068, -0.0013, -0.0053, -0.0275, -0.0101, -0.0025, -0.0302, -0.0031, -0.0102, -0.0075, -0.0046, -0.0018, -0.1352, -0.2894, -0.0031, -0.1318, -0.0044, -0.0027, -0.0025, -0.0062, -0.0068, -0.0031, -0.0013, -0.0029, -0.0029, -0.0016, -0.0566, -0.0466, -0.002, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0019, -0.0014, -0.0015, -0.0493], "cumulative_logprob": -2.2466, "norm_logp": -0.0522, "input_len": 479, "output_tokens": 43, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "338", "pred": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1.Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\n2.Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side down on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook another", "raw_output": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1.Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\n2.Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side down on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook another", "gt": "To make ribs, start by removing the membrane from the bottom of the ribs with a knife. Place the ribs in a resealable bag and add sufficient BBQ sauce to cover them. Afterwards, line a baking pan with heavy duty foil. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer for heat circulation on all sides. Mix mustard and liquid smoke, if using, and brush the ribs on both sides. Sprinkle the ribs with a dry rub and pat gently to make sure it adheres to the meat. To grill the ribs, heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium and remove the ribs from the foil. Place them meaty-side up on the grill and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook for another 5 minutes. Continue this process until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through with grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.", "question": "how to make ribs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.056, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 76, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1281, 55149, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 51325, 266, 264, 6819, 476, 60103, 39058, 311, 11051, 320, 18, 20, 15, 58472, 311, 220, 19, 20, 15, 58472, 4292, 17, 13270, 279, 55149, 504, 279, 46399, 11, 1992, 1105, 13041, 88, 24067, 1495, 389, 279, 39058, 11, 323, 15625, 1105, 448, 1045, 315, 279, 19187, 13, 17757, 279, 39058, 323, 4296, 369, 220, 20, 4420, 13, 40284, 323, 15625, 279, 55149, 323, 4296, 2441, 220], "logp_list": [-0.4448, -0.2785, -0.0372, -0.0689, -1.5047, -0.2215, -0.0225, -0.6045, -0.2059, -1.2987, -0.0004, -0.0634, -0.0166, -0.0027, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0019, -0.0045, -0.1137, -0.1593, -0.0118, -0.0001, -0.0082, -0.0033, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.9247, -0.0085, -0.1845, -0.0038, -0.0095, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0344, -0.0019, -0.0772, -0.0054, -0.0001, -0.0024, -0.2992, -0.0068, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.0087, -0.0008, -0.0009, -0.0085, -0.0011, -0.0012, -0.0016, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.5337, -0.052, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0012, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.3183, -0.0157, -0.0041, -0.0005, -0.0031, -0.0002, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0002], "cumulative_logprob": -7.6009, "norm_logp": -0.1, "input_len": 360, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 3.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 2.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "342", "pred": "No", "raw_output": "No", "gt": "While you may technically lose a small amount of weight after a bowel movement, this does not come from a loss of body fat. The decrease in body weight does not come from fat loss in stools, but rather from the elimination of undigested food and waste. Weight loss is primarily tied to your caloric intake and expenditure, not bowel movements. Unless your scale is highly accurate, you're unlikely to notice a difference in your body weight before and after a bowel movement.", "question": "when you have a bowel movement do you lose weight", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 1.213, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 1, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [2753], "logp_list": [-0.4631], "cumulative_logprob": -0.4631, "norm_logp": -0.4631, "input_len": 422, "output_tokens": 1, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "350", "pred": "The political, economic, and social effects of Reconstruction on the United States were significant. Politically, important changes accompanied the maintenance of national unity. Economically, the war brought economic unity to the US, with the South joining the market system and abandoning their former semi-subsistence economy. Socially, African Americans were technically free but faced challenges such as crop lien work contracts, segregation, and unresolved problems with the seceded states.", "raw_output": "The political, economic, and social effects of Reconstruction on the United States were significant. Politically, important changes accompanied the maintenance of national unity. Economically, the war brought economic unity to the US, with the South joining the market system and abandoning their former semi-subsistence economy. Socially, African Americans were technically free but faced challenges such as crop lien work contracts, segregation, and unresolved problems with the seceded states.", "gt": "The passages provided offer some insights into the political, economic, and social effects of various events in the history of the United States.\n\nPassage 1 highlights the impact of immigration on the country, indicating that it has shaped the nation since its early days. Immigration has contributed to the diversity of the population, cultural exchange, and the development of the economy.\n\nPassage 2 discusses the long-term effects of Reconstruction, which include the continuation of racial discrimination, segregation, and unequal access to employment opportunities for African Americans. This period saw the introduction of crop lien work contracts, which kept many African Americans in a state of virtual slavery.\n\nPassage 3 discusses the economic and political effects of the Civil War. The war brought economic unity to the country, as the South transitioned from a semi-subsistence economy to a market-based economy, and industrialization gained momentum. The passage also notes that important political changes accompanied the maintenance of national unity.\n\nBased on these passages, we can infer that the political, economic, and social effects of these historical events in the United States include:\n\n1. Immigration: Shaping the nation's population, culture, and economy.\n2. Reconstruction: Continued racial discrimination, segregation, and unequal access to employment opportunities for African Americans.\n3. Civil War: Economic unity, industrialization, and political changes that maintained national unity.\n\nTherefore, the political, economic, and social effects of these events in the United States include increased diversity, economic growth, and the continued struggle for racial equality.", "question": "what political, economic and social effects have on the united states", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 17.747, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 88, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 4948, 11, 6955, 11, 323, 3590, 6239, 315, 94694, 389, 279, 3639, 4180, 1033, 5089, 13, 15928, 2673, 11, 2989, 4344, 23943, 279, 13404, 315, 5313, 30326, 13, 50083, 2673, 11, 279, 4116, 7117, 6955, 30326, 311, 279, 2274, 11, 448, 279, 4882, 18169, 279, 3081, 1849, 323, 77239, 862, 4741, 18267, 1331, 15738, 80524, 8584, 13, 9784, 398, 11, 11643, 8877, 1033, 31554, 1910, 714, 16601, 11513, 1741, 438, 19097, 54420, 975, 17080, 11, 63144, 11, 323, 80161, 5322, 448, 279, 511, 1998, 291, 5302, 13], "logp_list": [-0.3272, -0.8378, -0.4929, -0.0061, -0.089, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0046, -0.2672, -0.8451, -0.5901, -0.0004, -0.003, -0.0, -0.728, -0.3811, -0.604, -0.0452, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.6916, -0.0013, -0.9506, -0.0006, -0.0008, -0.0013, -0.3142, -0.0003, -0.4515, -0.1189, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1702, -0.6579, -0.103, -0.0888, -0.0006, -0.0537, -0.0004, -0.1887, -0.2715, -0.6941, -0.0014, -0.0126, -0.0227, -0.0017, -0.0021, -0.0284, -0.06, -0.0797, -0.0475, -0.0251, -0.0362, -0.0016, 0.0, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.1047, -0.3345, -0.0002, -0.0182, -0.1798, -0.0044, -0.077, -0.3719, -0.0045, -0.5594, -0.1482, -0.3361, -0.9605, -0.0001, -0.4867, -0.0062, -0.0006, -0.0005, -0.7359, -0.0059, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.0212, -0.0551, -0.0041, -0.0302, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0216], "cumulative_logprob": -14.7741, "norm_logp": -0.1679, "input_len": 307, "output_tokens": 88, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 26.79, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "356", "pred": "Company goodwill is the reputation and connection with customers that tend to make that connection permanent. It represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation possesses. Goodwill is important in business because it will increase your customer base and retain old clients, attract investors and attract future buyers.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill is the reputation and connection with customers that tend to make that connection permanent. It represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation possesses. Goodwill is important in business because it will increase your customer base and retain old clients, attract investors and attract future buyers.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, company goodwill refers to the positive feelings and loyalty of customers towards a business, gained through providing good services or products, building trust, and creating a strong reputation. Goodwill is important because it can increase customer retention, attract new customers, and make the business more attractive to investors and potential buyers.\n\nAccording to passage 3, goodwill is an intangible asset that represents the value of a business's reputation and customer connections, which can make the connection between the business and its customers permanent.\n\nTherefore, goodwill is important for businesses because it can lead to increased customer loyalty, positive word-of-mouth advertising, and higher sales, ultimately contributing to the long-term success and growth of the business.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.039, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 59, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [14491, 81646, 374, 279, 17011, 323, 3633, 448, 6310, 429, 8376, 311, 1281, 429, 3633, 15330, 13, 1084, 10868, 279, 897, 315, 279, 32364, 311, 279, 6310, 892, 279, 829, 323, 17011, 49226, 13, 7684, 14387, 374, 2989, 304, 2562, 1576, 432, 686, 5263, 697, 6002, 2331, 323, 14060, 2310, 8239, 11, 9320, 14823, 323, 9320, 3853, 22630, 13], "logp_list": [-0.5691, -0.0343, -0.4646, -0.2769, -0.4717, -0.0945, -0.13, -0.1622, -0.0069, -0.8529, -1.1105, -0.0001, -0.0116, -0.763, -0.0172, -0.0033, -0.1704, -0.1137, -0.4629, -0.0422, -0.1507, -0.002, -0.0475, -0.0357, -0.0132, -0.1131, -0.0083, -0.1794, -0.0022, -0.0413, -0.0048, -0.0004, -0.0801, -0.0622, -1.0711, -0.007, -0.0354, -0.0176, -0.3653, -0.0016, -0.0987, -0.0035, -0.4551, -0.0094, -0.1936, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0796, -0.001, -0.001, -0.0019, -0.0101, -0.0188, -0.0004, -0.03, -0.0024, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0168], "cumulative_logprob": -8.9201, "norm_logp": -0.1512, "input_len": 294, "output_tokens": 59, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.55, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "360", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy:\n\nAd hominem fallacy refers to attacking an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. Poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, and it redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\n\nIn other words, ad hominem fallacy is a broader term that encompasses various types of attacks on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that focuses on the person's integrity or intelligence.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 14.574, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 67, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13], "logp_list": [-0.78, -0.4245, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0056, -0.0001, -0.3476, -0.1305, -0.1243, -0.2136, -0.1607, -0.0166, -0.0015, -0.0006, -0.9163, -0.0001, -0.07, -0.0018, -0.0101, -0.1138, -0.0001, -0.3572, -0.2596, -0.0001, -0.0875, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0437, -0.1701, -0.4708, -0.2041, -0.0025, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0, -1.142, -0.0337, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0102, -0.5242, -0.2486, -0.1036, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0083, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.8911, -0.0592, -0.0171, -0.0083, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0485], "cumulative_logprob": -8.0204, "norm_logp": -0.1197, "input_len": 244, "output_tokens": 67, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.36, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "368", "pred": "Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are from rocks. Rock can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand. On the other hand, stone is just a small piece or pebble that can be carried", "raw_output": "Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are from rocks. Rock can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand. On the other hand, stone is just a small piece or pebble that can be carried", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size. Rocks are typically larger and more massive than stones, which are smaller and more fragmented.\n\nPassage 1 states that rocks are often used to refer to something solid, large, and grounded, such as a mountain or palisade, while stones are often used to refer to small pieces of rock or pebbles. Passage 2 defines rocks as the hard material of the earth's crust that is exposed on the surface of the earth or underlying the soil, while stones are smaller fragments of rock that can be carried in the hand.\n\nPassage 3 further clarifies the distinction by stating that rocks are made up of smaller stones, while stones are made from rocks. Additionally, rocks are described as being difficult to carry in the hand, while stones are small enough to be carried easily.\n\nIn summary, while both rocks and stones are made of the same material, they differ in size and usage. Rocks are generally larger and more massive, while stones are smaller and more fragmented.", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.954, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 51, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [49, 25183, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 323, 26210, 525, 504, 23035, 13, 9127, 646, 387, 7481, 438, 264, 3460, 6573, 315, 9798, 429, 374, 5000, 311, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 13, 1913, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 9798, 374, 1101, 264, 2613, 6573, 476, 1051, 59880, 429, 646, 387, 11691], "logp_list": [-1.3765, -0.0009, -0.2863, -0.3606, -0.0035, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.034, -0.0009, -0.0066, -0.071, -0.0091, -0.0265, -0.8703, -0.0593, -0.005, -0.0056, -0.0038, -0.0026, -0.0047, -0.0011, -0.0021, -0.0008, -0.0141, -0.0022, -0.0031, -0.0004, -0.0045, -0.0016, -0.004, -0.0057, -0.0005, -0.0492, -0.0231, -0.0008, -0.0061, -0.0156, -0.0029, -0.0383, -0.0014, -0.0045, -0.0012, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.0034, -0.0003, -0.0017, -0.0158, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0023], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3383, "norm_logp": -0.0655, "input_len": 495, "output_tokens": 51, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.63, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "382", "pred": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. The cells in your worksheet highlight to confirm they're selected.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option.\n3. Click on", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. The cells in your worksheet highlight to confirm they're selected.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option.\n3. Click on", "gt": "Passage 3 provides the necessary information to answer the question. To arrange an excel sheet alphabetically, click on the Select All button, switch to the Data tab, locate the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option, and then select the desired column to sort by.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 13.997, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 72, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 30893, 458, 21029, 10834, 27790, 2673, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 9189, 389, 279, 8427, 2009, 3137, 518, 279, 19246, 315, 697, 2802, 323, 3250, 69452, 13, 576, 7761, 304, 697, 36636, 11167, 311, 7683, 807, 2299, 4091, 624, 17, 13, 15586, 311, 279, 2885, 5651, 304, 279, 5100, 21029, 44567, 323, 24523, 279, 15967, 609, 12339, 1874, 13, 9189, 389, 279, 15967, 2999, 624, 18, 13, 9189, 389], "logp_list": [-0.5105, -0.0461, -0.0081, -0.0891, -0.0067, -0.0602, -0.0001, -0.0726, -1.2465, -0.067, -0.0057, -0.336, -0.0373, -0.0008, -0.9881, -0.0064, -0.0154, -0.6178, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0808, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0014, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.1045, -0.118, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.3455, -0.0012, -0.0001, -0.002, -0.0069, -0.0, -0.2322, -0.0061, -0.0001, -0.0899, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0193, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.005, -0.0, -0.0, -0.6385, -0.012, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0128, -0.0375, -0.0034, -0.6592, -0.041, -0.0002], "cumulative_logprob": -6.543, "norm_logp": -0.0909, "input_len": 330, "output_tokens": 72, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.82, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "387", "pred": "The normal brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) test measures the amount of the BNP hormone in the blood", "raw_output": "Answer: The normal brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) test measures the amount of the BNP hormone in the blood", "gt": "The normal B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP) refers to a low amount of the BNP hormone found in the blood, produced by the heart as a measure of how well it is working. The exact normal values are not stated but are suggested to be a guide and can vary based on factors like age and gender, with values tending to increase with age and being higher in women. High levels of BNP are indicative of heart failure, with higher levels at diagnosis suggesting a more severe condition. Conversely, low levels tend to exclude heart failure.", "question": "what is the normal b natriuretic peptide", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 5.316, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 26, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 576, 4622, 8109, 308, 2361, 552, 28050, 71149, 320, 15594, 47, 8, 1273, 10953, 279, 3311, 315, 279, 425, 26227, 35808, 304, 279, 6543], "logp_list": [-0.9233, -0.0866, -0.408, -0.2341, -1.1427, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0114, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.486, -0.102, -0.0066, -0.0049, -0.0028, -0.0348, -0.0522, -0.0022, -0.0025, -0.0415, -0.2431, -0.0054], "cumulative_logprob": -4.7919, "norm_logp": -0.1843, "input_len": 456, "output_tokens": 26, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.54, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "400", "pred": "Bucharest has a humid continental climate. Winters can get windy and temperatures often dip below 0 °Celsius (32 °Fahrenheit), sometimes dropping to minus 20 °C (4 °F). In summer (July and August), the average temperature is around 23 °C (73 °F). Temperatures often reach 35 °C (95 °F) to 40 °C (104 °C) in mid-summer.", "raw_output": "Answer: Bucharest has a humid continental climate. Winters can get windy and temperatures often dip below 0 °Celsius (32 °Fahrenheit), sometimes dropping to minus 20 °C (4 °F). In summer (July and August), the average temperature is around 23 °C (73 °F). Temperatures often reach 35 °C (95 °F) to 40 °C (104 °C) in mid-summer.", "gt": "Based on passage 3, the average temperature in Bucharest, Romania during July and August is around 23 °C (73 °F). Temperatures can also reach 35 °C (95 °F) to 40 °C (104 °F) in mid-summer.", "question": "temperature bucharest", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 19.812, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 102, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 33278, 15432, 702, 264, 65938, 57536, 9977, 13, 467, 49940, 646, 633, 92921, 323, 19879, 3545, 23604, 3685, 220, 15, 36286, 34, 40247, 320, 18, 17, 36286, 37, 47910, 701, 7025, 25100, 311, 27283, 220, 17, 15, 36286, 34, 320, 19, 36286, 37, 568, 758, 7324, 320, 28427, 323, 6156, 701, 279, 5461, 9315, 374, 2163, 220, 17, 18, 36286, 34, 320, 22, 18, 36286, 37, 568, 74683, 2789, 3545, 5545, 220, 18, 20, 36286, 34, 320, 24, 20, 36286, 37, 8, 311, 220, 19, 15, 36286, 34, 320, 16, 15, 19, 36286, 34, 8, 304, 5099, 12, 62566, 13], "logp_list": [-0.8866, -0.0779, -0.0984, -0.0004, -0.4621, -0.0678, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0018, -0.0338, -0.1279, -0.0001, -0.0032, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0023, -0.0004, -0.0092, -0.0016, -0.0008, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0286, -0.0047, -0.1616, -0.0533, -0.3941, -0.0018, -0.0011, -0.1489, -0.1557, -0.0095, -0.0013, -0.001, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0015, -0.1085, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0072, -0.2873, -0.0492, -0.0008, -0.0063, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0005, -0.5489, -0.0007, -0.0213, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0993, -0.0002, -0.0255, -0.0183, -0.1637, -0.0, -0.0089, -0.0044, -0.023, -0.5512, -0.0013, -0.0008, -0.0042, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0172, -0.0074, -0.0017, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0185, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3497, -0.0073, -0.0014, -0.0001, -0.3664, -0.0077, -0.0308], "cumulative_logprob": -5.4881, "norm_logp": -0.0538, "input_len": 312, "output_tokens": 102, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.27, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "404", "pred": "To remove the rear door panel in your Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull and there's also one in the middle of the inside door handle. On the outer edge of the door there's also a pin the hold the panel in place, you will need to remove that. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "raw_output": "To remove the rear door panel in your Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull and there's also one in the middle of the inside door handle. On the outer edge of the door there's also a pin the hold the panel in place, you will need to remove that. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "gt": "The passage provides step-by-step instructions for removing the rear door panel on a 2006 Chevy HHR. To perform this task, the passenger must locate and remove the two screws inside the door pull cup and the middle of the inside door handle. A pin on the outside edge of the door must also be removed using a door panel removal tool. Once all these steps are completed, the door panel can be carefully pulled away from the frame, taking care not to damage any wiring connections.\n\nAccordingly, the answer to the question is:\n\nRemoving the rear door panel on a 2006 Chevy HHR involves locating and removing the screws inside the door pull cup and the middle of the inside door handle, as well as removing the pin on the outside edge of the door using a door panel removal tool.", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 20.52, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 86, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4057, 279, 14639, 6006, 6945, 304, 697, 57298, 472, 17088, 11, 498, 686, 1184, 311, 4057, 279, 1378, 44922, 7407, 4766, 315, 279, 6006, 6815, 323, 1052, 594, 1083, 825, 304, 279, 6149, 315, 279, 4766, 6006, 3705, 13, 1913, 279, 15955, 6821, 315, 279, 6006, 1052, 594, 1083, 264, 8983, 279, 3331, 279, 6945, 304, 1992, 11, 498, 686, 1184, 311, 4057, 429, 13, 5005, 990, 264, 6006, 6945, 16652, 5392, 311, 81279, 279, 6006, 6945, 1007, 315, 279, 472, 17088, 14639, 6006, 13], "logp_list": [-0.8951, -0.0085, -0.0037, -0.0574, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0302, -0.2324, -0.3569, -0.036, -0.0001, -0.0455, -0.0679, -0.009, -0.0009, -0.0009, -0.0277, -0.0351, -0.01, -0.0001, -0.0023, -0.0022, -0.0032, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0207, -1.2666, -0.0492, -0.0291, -0.0131, -0.0022, -0.0025, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.008, -0.0036, -0.2131, -0.2093, -0.0183, -0.0548, -0.0658, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0006, -0.0009, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.1617, -0.0066, -0.0014, -0.0012, -0.0006, -0.6877, -0.0578, -0.0012, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0265, -0.0074, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0025, -0.086, -0.0838, -0.0404, -0.0109, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0007, -0.0013, -0.0039, -0.0005, -0.0048, -0.0003, -0.0045, -0.0014, -0.0283], "cumulative_logprob": -5.0136, "norm_logp": -0.0583, "input_len": 512, "output_tokens": 86, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.85, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "409", "pred": "Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F. Place all of the ingredients into a blender carafe, cover and pulse until coarse crumbs form, about 2 minutes. Spread the crumbs evenly on 2 half sheet pans and bake until the crumbs just begin to brown and crisp, about 5 minutes. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, and then transfer to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "raw_output": "Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F. Place all of the ingredients into a blender carafe, cover and pulse until coarse crumbs form, about 2 minutes. Spread the crumbs evenly on 2 half sheet pans and bake until the crumbs just begin to brown and crisp, about 5 minutes. Cool the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, and then transfer to an airtight container and store at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\nPassage 1 states to push torn pieces of bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 2 provides similar instructions, stating to push chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300°F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 3 provides additional information, suggesting to preheat the oven to 300°F before making the crumbs. The passage also recommends using a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form, and then spreading the crumbs evenly on two half-sheet pans and baking until they just begin to brown and are crisp, about 5 minutes. After cooling the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, transfer them to an airtight container and store them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nTherefore, to make panko crumbs based on the provided passages, you can use a food processor or blender to shred or pulse bread into coarse crumbs, and then bake them in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted.", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 21.305, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 100, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4703, 19963, 279, 23387, 311, 220, 18, 15, 15, 12348, 434, 13, 10771, 678, 315, 279, 13966, 1119, 264, 61795, 1803, 5645, 11, 3421, 323, 27235, 3080, 49247, 96577, 1352, 11, 911, 220, 17, 4420, 13, 47716, 279, 96577, 41047, 389, 220, 17, 4279, 10834, 63783, 323, 22544, 3080, 279, 96577, 1101, 3161, 311, 13876, 323, 41854, 11, 911, 220, 20, 4420, 13, 23931, 279, 96577, 389, 279, 63783, 369, 220, 16, 20, 311, 220, 17, 15, 4420, 11, 323, 1221, 8317, 311, 458, 264, 2106, 491, 5476, 323, 3553, 518, 3054, 9315, 369, 705, 311, 220, 17, 5555, 13], "logp_list": [-1.4774, -0.0012, -0.7185, -0.0009, -0.0165, -0.0171, -0.1491, -0.0092, -0.0007, -0.0025, -1.1114, -0.0349, -0.0389, -0.0062, -0.0131, -0.0008, -0.0023, -0.006, -0.0013, -0.0004, -0.0031, -0.0003, -0.0095, -0.0009, -0.0386, -0.001, -0.0012, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0015, -0.0183, -0.0017, -0.0007, -0.0023, -0.0618, -0.0049, -0.0323, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0005, -0.0058, -0.0009, -0.0012, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.0035, -0.0006, -0.0013, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0021, -0.2343, -0.0076, -0.0012, -0.001, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.1012, -0.0741, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0014, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0013, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0097, -0.007, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0022, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0021, -0.0034, -0.0698, -0.0038, -0.0001, -0.0018, -0.0087], "cumulative_logprob": -4.351, "norm_logp": -0.0435, "input_len": 521, "output_toke