{"id": "413", "pred": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Sing Sing Monsters, you need to use the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.", "raw_output": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Sing Sing Monsters, you need to use the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.", "gt": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters, you need to obtain the Shugabush Monster from the other island and breed it with the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.", "question": "how do i breed a shugabeats in my singing monsters", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.874, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 27775, 264, 1417, 768, 8229, 1862, 304, 3017, 10833, 10833, 62708, 11, 498, 1184, 311, 990, 279, 1417, 768, 370, 1116, 323, 279, 49840, 38630, 304, 279, 1417, 768, 370, 1116, 10720, 39208, 5944, 13], "logp_list": [-0.1961, -0.0013, -0.0409, -0.0033, -0.0058, -0.0041, -0.0867, -0.1115, -0.0484, -0.0001, -0.1824, -0.0037, -0.026, -0.0994, -0.4687, -0.0204, -1.1267, -0.0188, -0.0011, -0.0065, -0.0009, -0.0044, -0.0181, -0.0118, -0.0128, -0.0015, -0.013, -0.0028, -0.006, -0.0025, -0.0011, -0.0027, -0.0036, -0.0009, -0.0015, -0.4262], "cumulative_logprob": -2.9617, "norm_logp": -0.0823, "input_len": 420, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 70.59, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "422", "pred": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other", "raw_output": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other", "gt": "A UPC description refers to the Universal Product Code, a barcode symbology used extensively in multiple countries including the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, and in Europe. It is used for tracking trade items in stores. UPC consists of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. It is primarily used for scanning of trade items at the point of sale, as per GS1 specifications.", "question": "what is a upc description", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.183, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 25500, 5643, 6119, 320, 3124, 34, 8, 374, 264, 49407, 66654, 2449, 429, 374, 13570, 1483, 304, 279, 3639, 4180, 11, 6864, 11, 3639, 15072, 11, 8330, 11, 1532, 16911, 11, 304, 4505, 323, 1008], "logp_list": [-1.153, -0.4727, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0725, -0.002, -0.0024, -0.021, -0.0555, -0.0096, -0.0029, -0.0032, -0.0, -1.1307, -0.0105, -0.0075, -0.0003, -0.0625, -0.0587, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0049, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0097, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0134, -0.0003, -0.0195, -0.0898], "cumulative_logprob": -3.2154, "norm_logp": -0.0893, "input_len": 390, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.23, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "428", "pred": "Twins can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs.", "raw_output": "Twins can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs.", "gt": "Twins are two offspring produced by the same pregnancy. They can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning they develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning they develop from two different eggs with each being fertilized by its own sperm cell. Monoamniotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs within 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic twin pregnancies are more likely in women of West African descent, especially Yoruba, aged between 30 and 40 years, of above average height and weight, and who have had several previous pregnancies.", "question": "how do twins happen", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.165, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 48, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 387, 2987, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 701, 7290, 429, 807, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 11, 892, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 11, 476, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 701, 7290, 429, 807, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 13], "logp_list": [-0.0546, -0.0, -0.5958, -0.8335, -0.0235, -0.0064, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1576, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.9025, -0.1192, -0.0346, -0.0017, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.004, -0.2746, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.071, -0.0812, -0.0029, -0.0019, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0103, -0.0009, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.004, -0.0082, -0.1016, -0.0, -0.008, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0245], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3339, "norm_logp": -0.0695, "input_len": 349, "output_tokens": 48, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.1, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "432", "pred": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "raw_output": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods based on the passages provided. For silver jewelry, you can mix mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth into the mixture, and rub the jewelry. Then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, you can make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, apply it to the silver with a soft cloth, and rinse it off. For diamonds, you can clean them with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat them dry with a soft cloth. If you have silver jewelry with gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.526, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 76, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [58083, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 568], "logp_list": [-2.2127, -0.167, -0.1472, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0053, -0.0018, -0.0091, -0.0031, -0.0002, -0.2067, -0.0209, -0.1891, -0.0017, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.2367, -0.0468, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0372, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0041, -0.0041, -0.0004, -0.0044, -0.0019, -0.0016, -0.0015, -0.0013, -0.0095, -0.0006, -0.0227, -0.5563, -0.0379, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0037, -0.0118, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0065, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0023, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0622, -0.0155, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0029, -0.0009, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.7873, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0002, -0.0012, -0.0594], "cumulative_logprob": -4.9278, "norm_logp": -0.0648, "input_len": 349, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.91, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "436", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "raw_output": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "gt": "To find someone in France, you can use the website www.pagesjaunes.fr, the French version of the white pages. Enter the last name of the person in the \"Nom\" field and optionally enter their first name in the \"Prenom\" field. If you know the address, you can enter it in the \"Ou\" section. Leave it blank if you don't know the address. Click on \"Trouver\" to search for the name. The search results will display the name, address, and phone number of the matching listings.", "question": "how to find someone in france", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.355, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 49, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1477, 4325, 304, 9625, 11, 498, 646, 3947, 8438, 37980, 5580, 8531, 18798, 11, 279, 21772, 2502, 82181, 11, 892, 374, 279, 8585, 2319, 315, 279, 4158, 6816, 13, 11252, 279, 1697, 594, 1537, 829, 304, 279, 36900, 2070, 13, 1446, 646, 3725, 264, 1156, 829, 304, 393], "logp_list": [-0.3293, -0.0043, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0229, -0.223, -0.0252, -1.6119, -0.7169, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.1457, -0.1807, -0.7629, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0865, -0.1373, -0.005, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.0018, -0.0098, -0.0003, -0.0076, -0.0154, -0.0039, -0.4859, -0.0397, -0.1079, -0.0524, -0.1201, -0.0006, -0.0824, -0.0028, -0.5882, -0.2827, -0.0061, -0.2117, -0.0209, -0.0018, -0.0018, -0.0186, -0.3196], "cumulative_logprob": -6.645, "norm_logp": -0.1356, "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 49, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.28, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "443", "pred": "Fire is the mechanism by which the forest is continually regenerated, states Hall. Fires consume dead, decaying vegetation accumulating on the forest floor, thereby clearing the way for new growth. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonon (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out", "raw_output": "Fire is the mechanism by which the forest is continually regenerated, states Hall. Fires consume dead, decaying vegetation accumulating on the forest floor, thereby clearing the way for new growth. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonon (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire helps to clear the way for new growth by consuming dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, creating a clean slate for seed germination and seedling establishment (Passage 2).\n2. Jack pine seeds are adapted to fire, as they are stored in serotinous cones that only open after a fire has melted the resin holding them closed. This allows the seeds to disperse and establish new populations (Passage 2).\n3. Fire can create a favorable environment for jack pine and aspen regrowth by reducing competition from other tree species, such as spruce, which have better access to sunlight while shading the other trees (Passage 3).\n\nTherefore, forest fires can benefit jack pines by providing a means of seed dispersal and creating conditions that promote their growth and regeneration.", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 18.806, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 96, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16697, 374, 279, 16953, 553, 892, 279, 13638, 374, 34511, 70353, 11, 5302, 10926, 13, 82830, 24057, 5593, 11, 1622, 17270, 53732, 91585, 389, 279, 13638, 6422, 11, 27492, 32750, 279, 1616, 369, 501, 6513, 13, 4329, 9419, 11, 1741, 438, 279, 25072, 33597, 11, 17188, 389, 3940, 311, 8865, 862, 19056, 13, 576, 25072, 33597, 18644, 1420, 23535, 263, 320, 416, 258, 43418, 8, 86892, 429, 525, 1602, 26128, 13, 576, 86892, 7146, 88360, 3080, 264, 3940, 13666, 323, 98091, 279, 53514, 13, 5005, 279, 86892, 2420, 1787, 323, 279, 19056, 4399, 476, 12244, 700], "logp_list": [-1.6295, -0.3618, -0.8945, -0.0008, -0.3423, -0.0002, -0.0077, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.235, -0.0466, -0.0035, -0.0157, -0.1604, -0.002, -0.0012, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0021, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0014, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0081, -0.2228, -0.008, -0.0036, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0013, -0.0016, -0.0033, -0.006, -0.014, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0013, -0.002, -0.0001, -0.0114, -0.3545, -0.0051, -0.0011, -0.0043, -0.0094, -0.3306, -0.24, -0.0542, -0.0027, -0.0002, -0.0119, -0.581, -0.0059, -0.0057, -0.5199, -0.0084, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0136, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0013, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0013, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.0026, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0017, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0005], "cumulative_logprob": -6.1674, "norm_logp": -0.0642, "input_len": 334, "output_tokens": 96, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 30.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "451", "pred": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while as craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "raw_output": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while as craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression created by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava empties, causing the overlying rocks to collapse. It is formed by the collapsing of the ground. On the other hand, a crater is a bowl-shaped structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava. It is formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure.", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.739, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 54, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 320, 266, 3245, 825, 13136, 3941, 701, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 1393, 438, 1560, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 11, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4024, -0.0755, -0.0004, -0.0313, -0.3122, -0.0203, -1.5601, -0.002, -0.0012, -0.0339, -0.0211, -0.0013, -0.0433, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0067, -0.0041, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0178, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.1919, -0.1136, -0.5906, -0.8889, -0.0005, -0.0131, -0.0247, -0.0003, -0.0019, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.1846, -0.0095, -0.017, -0.0056, -0.0018, -0.0024, -0.0018, -0.0011, -0.000