{"id": "619", "pred": "Step 2: Remove the printer from the devices list. Remove printers and uninstall any Windows print drivers using Windows Devices and Printers. If your printer connects to the computer with a USB cable, disconnect the cable from the printer. Turn the printer off and on again, and", "raw_output": "Step 2: Remove the printer from the devices list. Remove printers and uninstall any Windows print drivers using Windows Devices and Printers. If your printer connects to the computer with a USB cable, disconnect the cable from the printer. Turn the printer off and on again, and", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the information provided in the passages, here are the steps to force remove a printer in Devices and Printers:\n\n1. Open Print Manager or Printers folder.\n2. Right-click the printer you want to remove and select Delete. If you can't delete the printer, run the command with administrative privileges by right-clicking the printer again and selecting \"Run as administrator\" before deleting it.\n3. Alternatively, you can remove the printer from the devices list, uninstall the Windows print drivers, and disconnect the printer from the computer if it's connected via USB. Restart the Spooler Service after removing the printer or printer port.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "question": "how to force removal of a printer in devices and printers", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.63, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 55, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 17, 25, 10783, 279, 22380, 504, 279, 7611, 1140, 13, 10783, 55953, 323, 53635, 894, 5515, 1173, 11788, 1667, 5515, 40377, 323, 8213, 388, 13, 1416, 697, 22380, 33061, 311, 279, 6366, 448, 264, 11348, 14652, 11, 28969, 279, 14652, 504, 279, 22380, 13, 11999, 279, 22380, 1007, 323, 389, 1549, 11, 323],