{"id": "659", "pred": "To use vinegar for dogs, you can mix 1 cup of vinegar with 2-4 cups of water and use as a final rinse, spray vinegar full-strength on your dog's coat to normalize the pH balance of its skin, make a rinse to improve your dog's skin and coat, clean out your dog's ears using ACv, spray your dog's coat to repel fleas, bathe in ACV-soap mixture to fight fleas, use a flea comb to remove the fleas, rinse your", "raw_output": "To use vinegar for dogs, you can mix 1 cup of vinegar with 2-4 cups of water and use as a final rinse, spray vinegar full-strength on your dog's coat to normalize the pH balance of its skin, make a rinse to improve your dog's skin and coat, clean out your dog's ears using ACv, spray your dog's coat to repel fleas, bathe in ACV-soap mixture to fight fleas, use a flea comb to remove the fleas, rinse your", "gt": "Vinegar can be used to improve a dog's skin and coat, clean out their ears, repel fleas, and soothe itchy ears.", "question": "vinegar uses for dogs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 20.745, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 106, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 990, 46105, 369, 12590, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 220, 16, 10525, 315, 46105, 448, 220, 17, 12, 19, 25374, 315, 3015, 323, 990, 438, 264, 1590, 73241, 11, 22899, 46105, 2480, 5477, 17842, 389, 697, 5562, 594, 22875, 311, 21694, 279, 36043, 8172, 315, 1181, 6787, 11, 1281, 264, 73241, 311, 7269, 697, 5562, 594, 6787, 323, 22875, 11, 4240, 700, 697, 5562, 594, 24230, 1667, 10584, 85, 11, 22899, 697, 5562, 594, 22875, 311, 2064, 301, 12962, 300, 11, 15743, 383, 304, 10584, 53, 33019, 391, 20980, 311, 4367, 12962, 300, 11, 990, 264, 95855, 3614, 311, 4057, 279, 12962, 300, 11, 73241, 697], "logp_list": [-1.4783, -0.7604, -0.073, -0.5647, -0.1004, -0.1617, -0.5452, -0.0918, -0.5219, -0.2192, -0.0026, -0.0601, -0.0076, -0.0033, -0.0028, -0.0016, -0.0026, -0.0603, -0.0447, -0.0008, -0.0019, -0.0003, -0.3172, -0.0381, -0.1741, -0.0012, -0.0022, -0.0005, -1.9909, -0.1034, -0.0616, -0.0264, -0.0542, -0.0012, -0.0091, -0.047, -0.0057, -0.0362, -0.0007, -0.0939, -0.0071, -0.0164, -0.0323, -0.1416, -0.0018, -0.0086, -0.0003, -0.0325, -0.7485, -0.0075, -0.0002, -0.0073, -0.0004, -0.0082, -0.0018, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0106, -0.0002, -0.0102, -0.0805, -0.0046, -0.0012, -0.0007, -0.0009, -0.0006, -0.0081, -0.162, -0.147, -0.0092, -0.0232, -0.0037, -0.0009, -0.002, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0022, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0083, -0.0545, -0.0001, -0.0114, -0.1943, -0.0409, -0.0074, -0.0006, -0.0008, -0.0037, -0.0018, -0.0908, -0.0001, -0.0157, -0.513, -0.0096, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.0036, -0.015, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0701, -0.5782, -0.0046], "cumulative_logprob": -10.7832, "norm_logp": -0.1017, "input_len": 318, "output_tokens": 106, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.33, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "665", "pred": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "raw_output": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "gt": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. This forces air rich in carbon dioxide out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 23.807, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 120, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 59437, 1574, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 697, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13], "logp_list": [-0.6986, -0.123, -0.8764, -0.0002, -0.3657, -0.0999, -0.2992, -0.0004, -0.003, -0.0007, -0.2345, -0.0454, -0.0355, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0023, -0.0012, -0.5155, -0.1694, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0147, -0.0018, -0.0022, -0.0037, -0.004, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0813, -0.6247, -0.0206, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0104, -0.0012, -0.1803, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0022, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.454, -0.5371, -0.0237, -0.1967, -0.0056, -0.0609, -0.5405, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0058, -0.0175, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0442, -0.0075, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0059, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0044, -0.0004, -0.0102, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0082, -0.0202, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0025, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.001, -0.006, -0.0001, -0.0162, -0.5083, -0.0191, -0.0085, -0.0008, -0.0026, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0115], "cumulative_logprob": -6.9635, "norm_logp": -0.058, "input_len": 313, "output_tokens": 120, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 81.03, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "669", "pred": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "raw_output": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer based on the given passages:\n\nWhen you breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and pulls downwards, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to enter the lungs. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also contract, expanding the ribcage and further increasing the space in the chest cavity. Air enters the lungs through the windpipe (trachea) and fills the expanded lung tissue.\n\nWhen you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, allowing the chest cavity to decrease in size. As the space in the chest cavity decreases, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nTherefore, when you breathe in and out, air enters the lungs during inhalation and leaves the lungs during exhalation.", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 23.701, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 120, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 59437, 1574, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 697, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13], "logp_list": [-0.6986, -0.123, -0.8764, -0.0002, -0.3657, -0.0999, -0.2992, -0.0004, -0.003, -0.0007, -0.2345, -0.0454, -0.0355, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0023, -0.0012, -0.5155, -0.1694, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0147, -0.0018, -0.0022, -0.0037, -0.004, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0813, -0.6247, -0.0206, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0104, -0.0012, -0.1803, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0022, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.454, -0.5371, -0.0237, -0.1967, -0.0056, -0.0609, -0.5405, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0058, -0.0175, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0442, -0.0075, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0059, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0044, -0.0004, -0.0102, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0082, -0.0202, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0025, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.001, -0.006, -0.0001, -0.0162, -0.5083, -0.0191, -0.0085, -0.0008, -0.0026, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0115], "cumulative_logprob": -6.9635, "norm_logp": -0.058, "input_len": 313, "output_tokens": 120, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.2, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "675", "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is", "raw_output": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to your question:\n\nThe weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. The cost of goods available for sale is divided by the number of units available for sale to calculate the weighted-average cost per unit. This figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average method is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and cannot be assigned a specific cost, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 3.957, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 19, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374], "logp_list": [-0.6447, -0.2266, -0.0088, -0.0671, -0.3579, -0.4354, -0.0046, -0.155, -0.0755, -0.0001, -0.0032, -0.0124, -0.0027, -0.0613, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0264, -0.0019, -0.0003], "cumulative_logprob": -2.0839, "norm_logp": -0.1097, "input_len": 482, "output_tokens": 19, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.91, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "683", "pred": "To get a free VoIP service and phone number, you can visit the YouTube channel at http://www.youtube.com/user/ricsil2037. This video will show you how you can get a free Virtual Phone Number (VOIP). A voip number is a phone number that uses the internet to place and receive calls.", "raw_output": "To get a free VoIP service and phone number, you can visit the YouTube channel at http://www.youtube.com/user/ricsil2037. This video will show you how you can get a free Virtual Phone Number (VOIP). A voip number is a phone number that uses the internet to place and receive calls.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to get free VoIP service and phone numbers:\n\n1. Free DID (incoming numbers) from around the world: There are several companies offering free or paid VoIP services that provide incoming numbers from around the world. These numbers can be obtained by signing up for a VoIP service provider and paying a flat yearly rate or monthly fee.\n2. Get a free Virtual Phone Number (VOIP) from Google: According to the passage, Google offers free virtual phone numbers that can be used to place and receive calls using the internet. To get a free number, visit the author's YouTube channel and follow the instructions provided.\n3. Cloud VoIP Service: The passage mentions a cloud VoIP service that provides toll-free numbers and US local numbers that can be forwarded to any US number (including cell phones) or used with a SIP device. While there is no direct mention of free service, the passage does indicate that the pricing has been lowered.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. None of the passages provide detailed information on how to get free VoIP service and phone numbers without any additional costs or requirements.", "question": "how to get free voip service and phone number", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_s