{"id": "82", "pred": "Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea, constipation, alternating diarrhea and constipation, belly pains or cramps, and a lot of gas or bloating. The cause of IBS is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract.", "raw_output": "Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea, constipation, alternating diarrhea and constipation, belly pains or cramps, and a lot of gas or bloating. The cause of IBS is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract.", "gt": "Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Causes of irritable bowel syndrome are unknown but it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 17.167, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 79, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [27912, 51173, 315, 358, 7347, 2924, 68293, 11, 733, 48586, 11, 72362, 68293, 323, 733, 48586, 11, 35417, 50166, 476, 1560, 14647, 11, 323, 264, 2696, 315, 6819, 476, 14211, 1095, 13, 576, 5240, 315, 358, 7347, 374, 5023, 9788, 11, 714, 432, 374, 3381, 311, 1102, 504, 264, 10601, 315, 34563, 79211, 320, 28034, 8, 41829, 19029, 11, 7172, 17526, 315, 47031, 5746, 11, 323, 264, 43119, 304, 279, 10535, 1948, 279, 8109, 323, 279, 48509, 41829, 13], "logp_list": [-0.7401, -0.0005, -0.3969, -0.6126, -0.0005, -0.5442, -0.0743, -0.2327, -0.0215, -0.0, -0.0172, -1.1032, -0.4939, -0.0146, -0.002, -0.0001, -0.0033, -0.0836, -0.1272, -0.0573, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.069, -0.6902, -0.8165, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0013, -0.0065, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0436, -0.3157, -0.4205, -0.0163, -0.0654, -0.0, -0.002, -0.5901, -0.0026, -0.2093, -0.0232, -0.0132, -0.0075, -0.2814, -0.0013, -0.2027, -0.0, -0.0522, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0326, -0.095, -0.661, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0048, -0.1444, -0.0022, -0.0015, -0.0081, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0105, -0.0826, -0.0002, -0.0009, -0.0041, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0022, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0126], "cumulative_logprob": -9.4258, "norm_logp": -0.1193, "input_len": 335, "output_tokens": 79, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.58, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "88", "pred": "Place clean glass slide on a flat surface. Add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, and go at an angle of about 45 deg, touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make 2 smears, allow to air dry, and label clearly. Once", "raw_output": "Place clean glass slide on a flat surface. Add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, and go at an angle of about 45 deg, touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make 2 smears, allow to air dry, and label clearly. Once", "gt": "Sure, I'd be happy to help you with that! Here are the steps to prepare a blood slide based on the provided passages:\n\n1. First, sterilize a needle with a flame as described in Passage 1.\n2. Next, obtain a blood sample by pricking your finger or using a blood donation kit. This will require adult supervision, especially if you are doing it yourself.\n3. Place a drop of blood towards one end of a clean glass slide as instructed in Passage 2.\n4. Take another clean slide and, holding it at an angle of about 45 degrees, touch the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge of the slide through capillary action. Gently push the blood along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Repeat this step to make two smears.\n5. Allow the smears to air dry completely before labeling them clearly and placing them in the provided slide transport containers as suggested in Passage 3.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 18.561, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 93, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [17371, 4240, 8991, 14983, 389, 264, 10063, 7329, 13, 2691, 825, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 311, 825, 835, 13, 11778, 2441, 4240, 14983, 11, 323, 728, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 5254, 11, 5796, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 773, 279, 6543, 8473, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 13, 22950, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 9355, 13, 9646], "logp_list": [-1.726, -0.1554, -0.002, -0.0014, -0.0061, -0.0031, -0.0007, -0.0006, -0.144, -0.009, -0.01, -0.0019, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0071, -0.0009, -0.0003, -0.0129, -0.0348, -0.0003, -0.0025, -0.0003, -0.0261, -0.0212, -0.9257, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.0009, -0.0012, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0504, -0.02, -0.0012, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0051, -0.0009, -0.0002, -0.0361, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0016, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0009, -0.002, -0.0028, -0.0002, -0.0073, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0051, -0.0399, -0.0026, -0.0006, -0.0103, -0.1029, -0.0034, -0.001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0016, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0043, -0.0803], "cumulative_logprob": -3.4887, "norm_logp": -0.0375, "input_len": 320, "output_tokens": 93, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.28, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "92", "pred": "The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet, while nonessential", "raw_output": "The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet, while nonessential", "gt": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Some examples of nonessential amino acids are alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 6.71, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 32, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 773, 498, 1969, 633, 1105, 504, 697, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482], "logp_list": [-1.5124, -0.1792, -0.0079, -0.0027, -0.0041, -0.0001, -0.0165, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.289, -0.02, -0.1647, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.343, -0.0003, -0.4258, -0.0055, -0.0019, -0.0009, -0.6177, -0.0895, -0.0008, -0.0103, -0.0053, -0.0003, -0.0028, -0.0003, -0.1159, -0.1021, -0.0282, -0.0032], "cumulative_logprob": -3.9517, "norm_logp": -0.1235, "input_len": 497, "output_tokens": 32, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "97", "pred": "Average Weather in Indianapolis Indiana, United States. In", "raw_output": "Average Weather in Indianapolis Indiana, United States. In", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly and windy winters. The temperature ranges from 22°F to 85°F throughout the year. The passages also mention snowfall and snow accumulation in specific cities, but do not provide a comprehensive overview of the typical weather in Indiana. Therefore, it is not possible to provide a complete answer based solely on the given passages.", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 2.154, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 10, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [26292, 22629, 304, 41351, 21591, 11, 3639, 4180, 13, 758], "logp_list": [-2.5627, -0.2092, -0.0181, -0.0127, -0.1146, -0.0788, -0.0031, -0.0003, -0.0427, -0.1836], "cumulative_logprob": -3.2258, "norm_logp": -0.3226, "input_len": 327, "output_tokens": 10, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 7.41, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "103", "pred": "The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive and the nervous systems work together to help keep the body functioning normally.", "raw_output": "The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive and the nervous systems work together to help keep the body functioning normally.", "gt": "The various systems in the human body work together to ensure its normal functioning. For instance, the urinary system works with all other systems to clean the blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. This includes organs like the kidneys and lower intestines. Similarly, the respiratory system also collaborates with all other systems to provide oxygen to all body parts and remove carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. The respiratory system comprises the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "question": "how the body systems work together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.284, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 37, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 68297, 11, 51092, 11, 58971, 11, 41531, 11, 4225, 37120, 11, 71635, 11, 835, 77638, 11, 42645, 774, 11, 22077, 11, 41789, 323, 279, 22596, 5942, 975, 3786, 311, 1492, 2506, 279, 2487, 30201, 14279, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4445, -2.6672, -0.1113, -0.002, -0.0011, -0.0037, -0.0007, -0.0037, -0.0013, -0.0055, -0.0001, -0.0228, -0.0309, -0.0053, -0.0075, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0074, -0.0, -0.0016, -0.0035, -0.0112, -0.1429, -0.402, -0.3893, -0.0004, -0.2223, -0.425, -0.1125, -0.1426, -0.6006, -0.0319, -0.0073, -0.3879, -0.0008, -0.0053, -0.0427], "cumulative_logprob": -6.2459, "norm_logp": -0.1688, "input_len": 242, "output_t