{"id": "141", "pred": "When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 44(6) of the Income Tax Act, 1961 permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 44(6) of the Income Tax Act, 1961 permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nAccording to passage 1, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This reduction is allowed under section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act.\n\nPassage 3 further states that the assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even though such discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in such year and subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the block value of the asset by the scrap value in the previous year in which it is discarded, and to claim depreciation on the residual value of the asset in the year of discard and subsequent years.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.646, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 48, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4498, 458, 9329, 374, 43210, 553, 279, 2562, 714, 537, 6088, 11, 3772, 220, 19, 19, 7, 21, 8, 315, 279, 32520, 15190, 3218, 11, 220, 16, 24, 21, 16, 28690, 279, 20843, 897, 315, 279, 9329, 311, 387, 11046, 504, 279, 2504, 304, 279, 3681, 1042, 304], "logp_list": [-1.4536, -0.0066, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0013, -0.8514, -0.3528, -0.0013, -0.0887, -0.0115, -0.0001, -0.0099, -1.1302, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.2955, -0.0251, -0.019, -0.0317, -0.1399, -0.0029, -0.007, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.3989, -0.0024, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.2478, -0.0138, -0.005, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0057, -0.0014, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0311, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0038, -0.0037, -0.0018, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0251], "cumulative_logprob": -5.1752, "norm_logp": -0.1078, "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 48, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.4, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 2.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "150", "pred": "To bake hard-boiled eggs, place the cold eggs in a single layer in a pan with enough cold water to cover them by about 1 inch. Then, place the pan on the stove on high heat. As soon as the water comes to a boil turn the heat off, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat. Allow the eggs to sit in the water until they are cooked; about 9 minutes for a medium egg,", "raw_output": "To bake hard-boiled eggs, place the cold eggs in a single layer in a pan with enough cold water to cover them by about 1 inch. Then, place the pan on the stove on high heat. As soon as the water comes to a boil turn the heat off, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat. Allow the eggs to sit in the water until they are cooked; about 9 minutes for a medium egg,", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to bake hard-boiled eggs:\n\n1. Place cold eggs in a single layer in a pan with enough cold water to cover them by about 1 inch.\n2. Place the pan on the stove over high heat.\n3. As soon as the water comes to a boil, turn the heat off, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat.\n4. Allow the eggs to sit in the water for the appropriate amount of time, depending on the size of the eggs (9 minutes for medium, 12 minutes for large, and 15 minutes for extra-large).\n\nNote: Do not peek during the cooking process, and do not worry if the eggs are not perfectly symmetrical or have a slightly greenish-gray yolk.", "question": "how to bake hard boiled egg", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 17.909, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 90, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 22544, 2588, 69466, 2181, 18805, 11, 1992, 279, 9255, 18805, 304, 264, 3175, 6193, 304, 264, 7215, 448, 3322, 9255, 3015, 311, 3421, 1105, 553, 911, 220, 16, 17122, 13, 5005, 11, 1992, 279, 7215, 389, 279, 44015, 389, 1550, 8628, 13, 1634, 5135, 438, 279, 3015, 4041, 311, 264, 43690, 2484, 279, 8628, 1007, 11, 3421, 279, 7215, 11, 323, 4057, 432, 504, 279, 8628, 13, 26530, 279, 18805, 311, 2444, 304, 279, 3015, 3080, 807, 525, 29105, 26, 911, 220, 24, 4420, 369, 264, 11051, 18636, 11], "logp_list": [-1.6043, -0.184, -0.4772, -0.1032, -0.0002, -0.0037, -0.0374, -1.6842, -0.1636, -0.3523, -0.0008, -0.0033, -0.0006, -0.0289, -0.0006, -0.0024, -0.0005, -0.0029, -0.0083, -0.0016, -0.0309, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0621, -0.0013, -0.0004, -0.0048, -0.0007, -0.001, -0.0181, -0.1347, -0.0142, -0.0019, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.2737, -0.0091, -0.0006, -0.004, -0.0611, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.9751, -0.0073, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.0044, -0.0004, -0.0045, -0.0043, -0.0086, -0.0012, -0.0153, -0.0061, -0.0007, -0.0046, -0.007, -0.0025, -0.0125, -0.0029, -0.0011, -0.0004, -0.0423, -0.0009, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0078, -0.0021, -0.0186, -0.0064, -0.0435, -0.0044, -0.0011, -0.0016, -0.3931, -0.0191, -0.0087, -0.0047, -0.0043, -0.0085], "cumulative_logprob": -6.912, "norm_logp": -0.0768, "input_len": 390, "output_tokens": 90, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.04, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "166", "pred": "Prostate-specific antigen, or PSA, is a protein produced by cells of the prostate gland. The PSA test measures the level of PSA in a man's blood.", "raw_output": "Prostate-specific antigen, or PSA, is a protein produced by cells of the prostate gland. The PSA test measures the level of PSA in a man's blood.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, PSA levels refer to the amount of prostate-specific antigen present in a man's blood. The level of PSA is measured in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Normal PSA levels range between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml, and a moderate elevation in PSA levels may require further testing, such as a free PSA test, to determine the ratio of free to total PSA and help distinguish between prostate cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA. Therefore, the PSA test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer and to detect elevated PSA levels that may indicate prostate cancer or other prostate issues.", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.333, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 33, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1336, 2454, 18906, 81989, 11, 476, 69486, 11, 374, 264, 12833, 8947, 553, 7761, 315, 279, 46347, 66069, 13, 576, 69486, 1273, 10953, 279, 2188, 315, 69486, 304, 264, 883, 594, 6543, 13], "logp_list": [-2.3616, -0.003, -0.0194, -0.0011, -0.6731, -0.0066, -0.0006, -0.0038, -0.0281, -0.0113, -0.0386, -0.0089, -0.0004, -0.0184, -0.0029, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0285, -0.3221, -0.0182, -0.0069, -0.0061, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0006, -0.0009, -0.0011, -0.007, -0.0008, -0.0656, -0.0003, -0.0561], "cumulative_logprob": -3.6941, "norm_logp": -0.1119, "input_len": 459, "output_tokens": 33, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 19.8, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "172", "pred": "Preheat oven to lowest temperature, 150-200 degrees. Remove sugar from the package and put in an ovenproof container that will hold the sugar. Place in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon. If it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Always", "raw_output": "Preheat oven to lowest temperature, 150-200 degrees. Remove sugar from the package and put in an ovenproof container that will hold the sugar. Place in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon. If it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Always", "gt": "To soften sugar, place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil. Cover tightly. Remove damp paper towel in a couple of days. Alternatively, preheat oven to lowest temperature (150-200 degrees) and remove sugar from package. Put in an ovenproof container and place in the warm oven for 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container and confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location.", "question": "how to soften sugar", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.918, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 80, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4703, 19963, 23387, 311, 15457, 9315, 11, 220, 16, 20, 15, 12, 17, 15, 15, 12348, 13, 10783, 13163, 504, 279, 6328, 323, 2182, 304, 458, 23387, 15780, 5476, 429, 686, 3331, 279, 13163, 13, 10771, 304, 279, 8205, 23387, 369, 13187, 220, 16, 20, 4420, 13, 36134, 13163, 448, 264, 45505, 13, 1416, 432, 8471, 311, 4399, 10747, 11, 2484, 23387, 1007, 323, 5274, 13163, 304, 279, 23387, 369, 458, 6460, 476, 1378, 311, 6587, 9058, 700, 13, 23240], "logp_list": [-0.6305, -0.0004, -0.1443, -0.0012, -0.0296, -0.0063, -0.0224, -0.0094, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0076, -0.0048, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0009, -0.0153, -0.015, -0.0046, -0.0001, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0012, -0.0003, -0.0049, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.0099, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0016, -0.0018, -0.0015, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0011, -0.0009, -0.0034, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.013, -0.0021, -0.0073, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.007, -0.0191, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0105, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0556, -0.0013, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0015, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0003, -0.0017, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0087, -0.5783], "cumulative_logprob": -1.6414, "norm_logp": -0.0205, "input_len": 340, "output_tokens": 80, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "180", "pred": "Psychological effects of hugging include reducing worry of mortality, improving empathy and listening skills, and leading to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages.", "raw_output": "Answer: Psychological effects of hugging include reducing worry of mortality, improving empathy and listening skills, and leading to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages.", "gt": "Hugging has significant psychological effects. It creates a warm feeling that affects the entire body, akin to the effect of many drugs operating simultaneously. This could be due to the feeling of a loving embrace, leading to good relationships and emotional intimacy. It also enhances empathy and listening skills, which can lead to a psychological hug. Furthermore, research has found that hugging can significantly reduce worries about mortality and boost self-esteem. This soothing effect can be achieved even when hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear.", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 6.579, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 32, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 68969, 6239, 315, 305, 35268, 2924, 17719, 10955, 315, 28428, 11, 18392, 47351, 323, 14289, 7361, 11, 323, 6388, 311, 1661, 11871, 11, 14269, 65164, 11, 323, 6247, 46351, 13], "logp_list": [-1.1203, -0.0489, -0.777, -0.2678, -0.0212, -0.047, -0.0, -0.1348, -0.5314, -0.2353, -0.3772, -0.0003, -0.3354, -0.9427, -0.9188, -0.0842, -0.0059, -0.0005, -0.0076, -0.3905, -1.2497, -0.0001, -0.1839, -0.0006, -0.126, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0133, -0.0004, -0.0047, -0.0011, -0.0069], "cumulative_logprob": -7.8337, "norm_logp": -0.2448, "input_len": 264, "output_tokens": 32, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 30.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "185", "pred": "6 - 8 hours", "raw_output": "6 - 8 hours", "gt": "To make chicken tender in a crock pot, you can cook it on low for 6 - 8 hours or until done. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour, then on low for about 5 hours or until done. Another option is cooking it for 4 hours on high.", "question": "how long does it take to make chicken tender in crock pot", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 1.755, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 5, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [21, 481, 220, 23, 4115], "logp_list": [-0.6209, -0.2816, -0.0023, -0.0012, -0.0032], "cumulative_logprob": -0.9092, "norm_logp": -0.1818, "input_len": 485, "output_tokens": 5, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 17.14, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "194", "pred": "Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "raw_output": "Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to attach multiple picture frames together:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding (Passage 1).\n2. Lay the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect (Passage 1).\n3. Mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides of the frames, making sure the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame (Passage 1).\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames (Passage 1).\n5. Test ideas by laying the group of multiple pictures out on the floor first (Passage 2).\n6. Screw the frame to the backer board, repeat with each frame, and attach a hanging wire to the back (Passage 3).\n\nIt's important to note that the passages do not provide specific information on how to attach multiple frames together other than the steps mentioned above. Therefore, if more detailed instructions are needed, it may be best to consult additional resources or seek advice from a professional framer.", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 5.822, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 12, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [30485, 1378, 2613, 79834, 311, 279, 20913, 11067, 315, 279, 14087, 13], "logp_list": [-0.9318, -0.23, -0.0381, -0.0002, -0.0078, -0.0023, -0.3973, -0.0006, -0.0013, -0.0068, -0.0096, -0.1204], "cumulative_logprob": -1.7462, "norm_logp": -0.1455, "input_len": 471, "output_tokens": 12, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 10.94, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "203", "pred": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. Some of these bacteria also cause infections such as strep throat and impetigo. Usually", "raw_output": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. Some of these bacteria also cause infections such as strep throat and impetigo. Usually", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main cause of necrotizing fasciitis is bacterial infection, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus. Additionally, other types of bacteria have also been associated with the disease. Trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, can also play a role in the development of necrotizing fasciitis. Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures can also serve as a source of infection. Therefore, the answer is: Bacterial infection, including group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus, and trauma.", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 6.758, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 33, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [45, 757, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 374, 8881, 553, 3807, 12829, 315, 23157, 13, 4329, 315, 1493, 23157, 1083, 5240, 28920, 1741, 438, 5412, 79, 27591, 323, 3163, 295, 7836, 13, 32967], "logp_list": [-0.8143, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.1617, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1133, -0.2699, -0.0109, -0.6044, -0.0433, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.7984, -0.4523, -0.0022, -0.0053, -0.0015, -0.0147, -0.0001, -0.0293, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0043, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.019, -0.4492], "cumulative_logprob": -3.7958, "norm_logp": -0.115, "input_len": 520, "output_tokens": 33, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 12.66, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 4.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "207", "pred": "chard is a beet that", "raw_output": "chard is a beet that", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is what can be inferred about the vegetable \"chard\":\n\n* Chard is a type of beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) that has been chosen for leaf production over storage root formation.\n* Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n* The flavor of chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive taste (some consider it bitter).\n* Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce bitterness and bring out a refined flavor.\n* Chard is an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden.\n* Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator to minimize spoilage.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is chard vegetable?\" is: A beet variety (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) grown for leaf production, characterized by large, fleshy leaves and stalks with a strong, bitter flavor that becomes more refined with cooking.", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 1.607, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 6, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [331, 567, 374, 264, 48012, 429], "logp_list": [-0.8295, -0.0001, -0.7496, -0.1023, -0.498, -0.5266], "cumulative_logprob": -2.7061, "norm_logp": -0.451, "input_len": 423, "output_tokens": 6, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.6, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "212", "pred": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. In other words", "raw_output": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. In other words", "gt": "Here are my answers to the question based on the provided passages:\n\nQ: What are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures?\nA: Based on the passages provided, here are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures:\n\nElements:\n\n* Are the purest form of a chemical substance (Passage 3)\n* Consist of only one type of atom with the same number of protons (Passage 3)\n\nCompounds:\n\n* Are substances composed of two or more different elements (Passage 3)\n* Are also pure substances (Passage 3)\n\nMixtures:\n\n* Contain two or more substances that are relatively easy to separate (Passage 1)\n* Have a variable composition (Passage 2)\n* Show the properties of their constituent substances (Passage 2)\n* Can be separated by physical means (Passage 2)", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 4.687, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 23, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [11868, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 323, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 13, 758, 1008, 4244], "logp_list": [-1.1642, -0.6867, -0.3932, -0.012, -0.0009, -0.0032, -0.0021, -0.0132, -0.0009, -0.0024, -0.0945, -0.0527, -0.0017, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0205, -0.3906, -0.1473, -0.1635], "cumulative_logprob": -3.1521, "norm_logp": -0.137, "input_len": 415, "output_tokens": 23, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 21.28, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "216", "pred": "To wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n\n1. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adher", "raw_output": "To wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n\n1. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adher", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the steps to properly wash wine glasses:\n\n1. If hot water and mild detergent cannot remove wine residue, use baking soda to dissolve and loosen the adhesive residue (Passage 1).\n2. To clean the inside of a glass, use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles and rinse thoroughly with hot water (Passage 2).\n3. For hand cleaning, lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water and wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage (Passage 3). Use a small amount of detergent and only in the bottom of the glass and around the rim to avoid flavoring future wine. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how to wash wine glasses properly", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.843, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 65, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 11369, 13078, 28147, 10277, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1416, 4017, 3015, 323, 23034, 90134, 646, 944, 633, 697, 28147, 4240, 11, 990, 27815, 38862, 311, 4057, 894, 51396, 13078, 48132, 13, 51032, 3948, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 38862, 448, 4017, 3015, 323, 1077, 432, 2498, 304, 279, 8991, 369, 264, 2421, 4420, 13, 576, 38862, 686, 72639, 894, 32147, 13078, 34951], "logp_list": [-0.9255, -0.5418, -0.0245, -0.0002, -0.0083, -0.0197, -1.0623, -0.0433, -0.0405, -0.5628, -0.0505, -0.0032, -1.1422, -0.0689, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0028, -0.0001, -0.1504, -0.0165, -0.1155, -0.0662, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0242, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0118, -0.0002, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.3788, -0.0237, -0.0001, -0.0029, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0014, -0.0044, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0123, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0031, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.055, -0.0333, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0004, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.0239], "cumulative_logprob": -5.4348, "norm_logp": -0.0836, "input_len": 328, "output_tokens": 65, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 37.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "225", "pred": "Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet. Step 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arm. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm. Step 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The then end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If", "raw_output": "Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet. Step 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arm. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm. Step 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The then end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how to fix a lift valve in a toilet:\n\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet before starting the repair (Passage 1).\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms, and remove any remaining broken lift chain from the lift arm (Passage 1).\n3. Open the water valve to fill the tank and flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If necessary, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links (Passage 2).\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, which is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm (Passage 3). Secure it using pliers to ensure it is tightly fastened.\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test the toilet's flush function. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm (Passage 3).\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide instructions on how to fix a specific issue with the lift valve, such as a stuck or leaking valve.", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 42.599, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 212, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 1417, 27440, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 311, 279, 26214, 13, 14822, 220, 17, 25, 10783, 279, 2310, 1320, 3106, 31430, 553, 63702, 432, 1007, 279, 33639, 6773, 13, 10783, 1128, 594, 2115, 315, 279, 10865, 11893, 8781, 504, 279, 11893, 6773, 13, 14822, 220, 19, 25, 5264, 279, 3015, 31430, 311, 5155, 279, 12896, 13, 57626, 279, 26214, 311, 1779, 369, 4396, 5666, 13, 1416, 4362, 11, 1349, 4250, 279, 11893, 8781, 553, 7218, 279, 9317, 12327, 705, 476, 1495, 264, 5625, 315, 7746, 13, 3197, 498, 3129, 19527, 11, 3931, 1007, 279, 13623, 11893, 8781, 311, 5358, 432, 504, 3709, 92841, 13, 22950, 279, 2613, 6437, 835, 315, 279, 26214, 11893, 6773, 1119, 279, 18198, 3705, 13, 1416, 264, 21966, 374, 5230, 448, 697, 949, 11, 21966, 432, 304, 38969, 13, 22950,