{"id": "351", "pred": "Company goodwill is the reputation and connection with customers that tend to make that connection permanent. It represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation possesses. Goodwill is important in business because it will increase your customer base and retain old clients, attract investors and attract future buyers.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill is the reputation and connection with customers that tend to make that connection permanent. It represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation possesses. Goodwill is important in business because it will increase your customer base and retain old clients, attract investors and attract future buyers.", "gt": "Company goodwill is a business asset that is created when a business provides good service or product to their customers, leading to customer trust. This trust results in repeat customers and positive word-of-mouth, which can increase a company's customer base and retain old clients. Goodwill represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation of the business possesses. It is important because it can attract investors and future buyers, and it is an advantage of the reputation and connection with customers which tends to make that connection permanent.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.025, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 59, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [14491, 81646, 374, 279, 17011, 323, 3633, 448, 6310, 429, 8376, 311, 1281, 429, 3633, 15330, 13, 1084, 10868, 279, 897, 315, 279, 32364, 311, 279, 6310, 892, 279, 829, 323, 17011, 49226, 13, 7684, 14387, 374, 2989, 304, 2562, 1576, 432, 686, 5263, 697, 6002, 2331, 323, 14060, 2310, 8239, 11, 9320, 14823, 323, 9320, 3853, 22630, 13], "logp_list": [-0.5691, -0.0343, -0.4646, -0.2769, -0.4717, -0.0945, -0.13, -0.1622, -0.0069, -0.8529, -1.1105, -0.0001, -0.0116, -0.763, -0.0172, -0.0033, -0.1704, -0.1137, -0.4629, -0.0422, -0.1507, -0.002, -0.0475, -0.0357, -0.0132, -0.1131, -0.0083, -0.1794, -0.0022, -0.0413, -0.0048, -0.0004, -0.0801, -0.0622, -1.0711, -0.007, -0.0354, -0.0176, -0.3653, -0.0016, -0.0987, -0.0035, -0.4551, -0.0094, -0.1936, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0796, -0.001, -0.001, -0.0019, -0.0101, -0.0188, -0.0004, -0.03, -0.0024, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0168], "cumulative_logprob": -8.9201, "norm_logp": -0.1512, "input_len": 294, "output_tokens": 59, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.88, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "357", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "gt": "An ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character, motive, or other personal attribute of the person making an argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. This is often done to invalidate their arguments by associating their character with the soundness of their argument. On the other hand, the poisoning the well fallacy, which is a type of ad hominem attack, occurs when a person's integrity or intelligence is attacked before the merits of a case can be considered, effectively redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 14.709, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 67, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13], "logp_list": [-0.78, -0.4245, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0056, -0.0001, -0.3476, -0.1305, -0.1243, -0.2136, -0.1607, -0.0166, -0.0015, -0.0006, -0.9163, -0.0001, -0.07, -0.0018, -0.0101, -0.1138, -0.0001, -0.3572, -0.2596, -0.0001, -0.0875, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0437, -0.1701, -0.4708, -0.2041, -0.0025, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0, -1.142, -0.0337, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0102, -0.5242, -0.2486, -0.1036, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0083, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.8911, -0.0592, -0.0171, -0.0083, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0485], "cumulative_logprob": -8.0204, "norm_logp": -0.1197, "input_len": 244, "output_tokens": 67, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 63.74, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "361", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "gt": "The main difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is the timing of the attack. Ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument instead of attacking the argument directly, but poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive attack on a person's character or integrity before they have even made their argument. In other words, ad hominem fallacy is a reaction to an argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive strike to discredit an opponent before they have a chance to present their argument.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.048, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 67, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13], "logp_list": [-0.78, -0.4245, -0.0017, -0.0001, -0.0056, -0.0001, -0.3476, -0.1305, -0.1243, -0.2136, -0.1607, -0.0166, -0.0015, -0.0006, -0.9163, -0.0001, -0.07, -0.0018, -0.0101, -0.1138, -0.0001, -0.3572, -0.2596, -0.0001, -0.0875, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0437, -0.1701, -0.4708, -0.2041, -0.0025, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0, -1.142, -0.0337, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0102, -0.5242, -0.2486, -0.1036, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0083, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.8911, -0.0592, -0.0171, -0.0083, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0485], "cumulative_logprob": -8.0204, "norm_logp": -0.1197, "input_len": 244, "output_tokens": 67, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.96, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "375", "pred": "DMSO is used as an antifreeze and as a solvent for a wide range of chemicals. There are also a wide range of medical uses. It has a remarkable capacity to penetrate", "raw_output": "DMSO is used as an antifreeze and as a solvent for a wide range of chemicals. There are also a wide range of medical uses. It has a remarkable capacity to penetrate", "gt": "DMSO, or dimethyl sulfoxide, is used in a variety of ways both industrially and medically. Industrially, it is used as an antifreeze and a solvent for numerous chemicals. Medically, DMSO is applied to the skin as an anti-inflammatory and general pain reliever, most commonly for muscles and joints. It is also used to promote healing for minor cuts, burns, soft tissue damage, local tissue death, and skin ulcers. DMSO has the unique ability to penetrate skin and tissue, allowing it to enter the bloodstream.", "question": "what is dmso used for", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.807, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 38, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [35, 4826, 46, 374, 1483, 438, 458, 3196, 333, 27633, 323, 438, 264, 68896, 369, 264, 6884, 2088, 315, 25266, 13, 2619, 525, 1083, 264, 6884, 2088, 315, 6457, 5711, 13, 1084, 702, 264, 22804, 8654, 311, 63755], "logp_list": [-0.4309, -0.0011, -0.0004, -0.551, -0.1236, -1.0938, -0.0882, -0.0557, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.9023, -0.4435, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0772, -0.0248, -0.0046, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.4049, -1.7416, -0.0289, -0.061, -0.0124, -0.0012, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.1241, -0.4209, -0.2908, -0.0176, -0.0012, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0014], "cumulative_logprob": -6.9076, "norm_logp": -0.1818, "input_len": 301, "output_tokens": 38, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 23.88, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "383", "pred": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. The cells in your worksheet highlight to confirm they're selected.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option.\n3. Click on", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. The cells in your worksheet highlight to confirm they're selected.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option.\n3. Click on", "gt": "To arrange an excel sheet alphabetical, one can follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the Data tab from the toolbar at the top of the screen.\n2. Click on the Sort button and select Custom Sort from the popup menu.\n3. When the Sort window appears, select the data that you wish to sort by. In this example, we want to sort by the Product column (column B) in alphabetical order (A to Z).\n4. Click on the OK button.\n5. The data should now be sorted in alphabetical order.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 14.239, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 72, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 30893, 458, 21029, 10834, 27790, 2673, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 9189, 389, 279, 8427, 2009, 3137, 518, 279, 19246, 315, 697, 2802, 323, 3250, 69452, 13, 576, 7761, 304, 697, 36636, 11167, 311, 7683, 807, 2299, 4091, 624, 17, 13, 15586, 311, 279, 2885, 5651, 304, 279, 5100, 21029, 44567, 323, 24523, 279, 15967, 609, 12339, 1874, 13, 9189, 389, 279, 15967, 2999, 624, 18, 13, 9189, 389], "logp_list": [-0.5105, -0.0461, -0.0081, -0.0891, -0.0067, -0.0602, -0.0001, -0.0726, -1.2465, -0.067, -0.0057, -0.336, -0.0373, -0.0008, -0.9881, -0.0064, -0.0154, -0.6178, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0808, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0014, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.1045, -0.118, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.3455, -0.0012, -0.0001, -0.002, -0.0069, -0.0, -0.2322, -0.0061, -0.0001, -0.0899, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0004, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0193, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.005, -0.0, -0.0, -0.6385, -0.012, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0128, -0.0375, -0.0034, -0.6592, -0.041, -0.0002], "cumulative_logprob": -6.543, "norm_logp": -0.0909, "input_len": 330, "output_tokens": 72, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.69, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 3.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "388", "pred": "The normal brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) test measures the amount of the BNP hormone in the blood", "raw_output": "Answer: The normal brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) test measures the amount of the BNP hormone in the blood", "gt": "The normal B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) level is not specifically mentioned in the given passages. Therefore, it is unable to answer based on the given passages.", "question": "what is the normal b natriuretic peptide", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 5.428, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 26, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 576, 4622, 8109, 308, 2361, 552, 28050, 71149, 320, 15594, 47, 8, 1273, 10953, 279, 3311, 315, 279, 425, 26227, 35808, 304, 279, 6543], "logp_list": [-0.9233, -0.0866, -0.408, -0.2341, -1.1427, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0114, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.486, -0.102, -0.0066, -0.0049, -0.0028, -0.0348, -0.0522, -0.0022, -0.0025, -0.0415, -0.2431, -0.0054], "cumulative_logprob": -4.7919, "norm_logp": -0.1843, "input_len": 456, "output_tokens": 26, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "401", "pred": "To remove the rear door panel in your Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull and there's also one in the middle of the inside door handle. On the outer edge of the door there's also a pin the hold the panel in place, you will need to remove that. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "raw_output": "To remove the rear door panel in your Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull and there's also one in the middle of the inside door handle. On the outer edge of the door there's also a pin the hold the panel in place, you will need to remove that. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "gt": "To remove the door panel on a 2006 Chevy, first remove the two screws located inside of the door pull cup and one in the middle of the inside door handle. Additionally, there's a pin on the outer edge of the door that holds the panel in place which needs to be removed. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off. Ensure to remove all screws and clips in the door panel and gently pull the panel away from the frame. Finally, remove any wiring connections and the panel will come off the frame.", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 21.35, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 86, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4057, 279, 14639, 6006, 6945, 304, 697, 57298, 472, 17088, 11, 498, 686, 1184, 311, 4057, 279, 1378, 44922, 7407, 4766, 315, 279, 6006, 6815, 323, 1052, 594, 1083, 825, 304, 279, 6149, 315, 279, 4766, 6006, 3705, 13, 1913, 279, 15955, 6821, 315, 279, 6006, 1052, 594, 1083, 264, 8983, 279, 3331, 279, 6945, 304, 1992, 11, 498, 686, 1184, 311, 4057, 429, 13, 5005, 990, 264, 6006, 6945, 16652, 5392, 311, 81279, 279, 6006, 6945, 1007, 315, 279, 472, 17088, 14639, 6006, 13], "logp_list": [-0.8951, -0.0085, -0.0037, -0.0574, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0302, -0.2324, -0.3569, -0.036, -0.0001, -0.0455, -0.0679, -0.009, -0.0009, -0.0009, -0.0277, -0.0351, -0.01, -0.0001, -0.0023, -0.0022, -0.0032, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0207, -1.2666, -0.0492, -0.0291, -0.0131, -0.0022, -0.0025, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.008, -0.0036, -0.2131, -0.2093, -0.0183, -0.0548, -0.0658, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0006, -0.0009, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.1617, -0.0066, -0.0014, -0.0012, -0.0006, -0.6877, -0.0578, -0.0012, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0265, -0.0074, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0025, -0.086, -0.0838, -0.0404, -0.0109, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0007, -0.0013, -0.0039, -0.0005, -0.0048, -0.0003, -0.0045, -0.0014, -0.0283], "cumulative_logprob": -5.0136, "norm_logp": -0.0583, "input_len": 512, "output_tokens": 86, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.5, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "405", "pred": "To remove the rear door panel in your Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull and there's also one in the middle of the inside door handle. On the outer edge of the door there's also a pin the hold the panel in place, you will need to remove that. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "raw_output": "To remove the rear door panel in your Chevy HHR, you will need to remove the two screws located inside of the door pull and there's also one in the middle of the inside door handle. On the outer edge of the door there's also a pin the hold the panel in place, you will need to remove that. Then use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nTo remove the rear door panel in a 2006 Chevy HHR, you should follow these steps:\n\n1. Remove the two screws located inside the door pull cup.\n2. Remove the screw located in the middle of the inside door handle.\n3. Remove the pin on the outer edge of the door that holds the panel in place.\n4. Use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.\n\nIt is important to note that an instructional video is also available that shows the entire process and location of the screws and pin.", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 21.032, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 86, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4057, 279, 14639, 6006, 6945, 304, 697, 57298, 472, 17088, 11, 498, 686, 1184, 311, 4057, 279, 1378, 44922, 7407, 4766, 315, 279, 6006, 6815, 323, 1052, 594, 1083, 825, 304, 279, 6149, 315, 279, 4766, 6006, 3705, 13, 1913, 279, 15955, 6821, 315, 279, 6006, 1052, 594, 1083, 264, 8983, 279, 3331, 279, 6945, 304, 1992, 11,