{"id": "433", "pred": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "raw_output": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to clean tarnished jewelry at home:\n\n1. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth to shine silver jewelry that is merely dull or starting to discolor. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\n2. Use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth.\n3. Soak tarnished silver jewelry in a saltwater solution of two cups of hot water. This method is gentle and can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Make sure the gemstones will not be affected by the solution before submerging them.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to clean other types of jewelry, such as gold or platinum, or what to do if the tarnish is heavy or if the jewelry has intricate designs that may be damaged during cleaning.", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.916, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 76, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [58083, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 568], "logp_list": [-2.2127, -0.167, -0.1472, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0053, -0.0018, -0.0091, -0.0031, -0.0002, -0.2067, -0.0209, -0.1891, -0.0017, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.2367, -0.0468, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0372, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0041, -0.0041, -0.0004, -0.0044, -0.0019, -0.0016, -0.0015, -0.0013, -0.0095, -0.0006, -0.0227, -0.5563, -0.0379, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0037, -0.0118, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0065, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0023, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0622, -0.0155, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0029, -0.0009, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.7873, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0002, -0.0012, -0.0594], "cumulative_logprob": -4.9278, "norm_logp": -0.0648, "input_len": 349, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.84, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "438", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "raw_output": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the steps to find someone in France:\n\n1. Check the French White Pages (in English): You can use the website pagesjaunes.fr to search for people in France. To search for someone in Paris or its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the department or region field instead of typing Paris.\n2. Use the nom field to enter the last name of the person you are looking for, and you can also include their first name in the prenom field.\n3. Provide address information, if known, in the ou section to refine your search.\n4. Click \"trouver\" to search for the name, and the search results will display name, address, and phone number for all listings that match your search criteria.\n\nNote that the effectiveness of these methods may depend on how up-to-date the information in the white pages is and whether the person you are looking for has listed their contact information publicly.", "question": "how to find someone in france", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.519, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 49, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1477, 4325, 304, 9625, 11, 498, 646, 3947, 8438, 37980, 5580, 8531, 18798, 11, 279, 21772, 2502, 82181, 11, 892, 374, 279, 8585, 2319, 315, 279, 4158, 6816, 13, 11252, 279, 1697, 594, 1537, 829, 304, 279, 36900, 2070, 13, 1446, 646, 3725, 264, 1156, 829, 304, 393], "logp_list": [-0.3293, -0.0043, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0229, -0.223, -0.0252, -1.6119, -0.7169, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.1457, -0.1807, -0.7629, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0865, -0.1373, -0.005, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.0018, -0.0098, -0.0003, -0.0076, -0.0154, -0.0039, -0.4859, -0.0397, -0.1079, -0.0524, -0.1201, -0.0006, -0.0824, -0.0028, -0.5882, -0.2827, -0.0061, -0.2117, -0.0209, -0.0018, -0.0018, -0.0186, -0.3196], "cumulative_logprob": -6.645, "norm_logp": -0.1356, "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 49, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.01, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "444", "pred": "Fire is the mechanism by which the forest is continually regenerated, states Hall. Fires consume dead, decaying vegetation accumulating on the forest floor, thereby clearing the way for new growth. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonon (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out", "raw_output": "Fire is the mechanism by which the forest is continually regenerated, states Hall. Fires consume dead, decaying vegetation accumulating on the forest floor, thereby clearing the way for new growth. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonon (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines because they help to clear away dead vegetation and promote new growth. According to passage 2, the jack pine produces serotonous cones that are dormant until a fire occurs, at which point they melt and release their seeds. This suggests that fire is a mechanism for the dispersal of jack pine seeds, and that the tree relies on fires to spread its seeds. Additionally, passage 3 suggests that while spruce trees may outcompete jack pine and aspen in some areas, fires can disrupt this cycle and allow jack pine and aspen to regain an advantage. Therefore, it seems that fires play a role in promoting the growth and survival of jack pine and other pioneer species in the boreal forest.", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 19.111, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 96, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16697, 374, 279, 16953, 553, 892, 279, 13638, 374, 34511, 70353, 11, 5302, 10926, 13, 82830, 24057, 5593, 11, 1622, 17270, 53732, 91585, 389, 279, 13638, 6422, 11, 27492, 32750, 279, 1616, 369, 501, 6513, 13, 4329, 9419, 11, 1741, 438, 279, 25072, 33597, 11, 17188, 389, 3940, 311, 8865, 862, 19056, 13, 576, 25072, 33597, 18644, 1420, 23535, 263, 320, 416, 258, 43418, 8, 86892, 429, 525, 1602, 26128, 13, 576, 86892, 7146, 88360, 3080, 264, 3940, 13666, 323, 98091, 279, 53514, 13, 5005, 279, 86892, 2420, 1787, 323, 279, 19056, 4399, 476, 12244, 700], "logp_list": [-1.6295, -0.3618, -0.8945, -0.0008, -0.3423, -0.0002, -0.0077, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.235, -0.0466, -0.0035, -0.0157, -0.1604, -0.002, -0.0012, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0021, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0014, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0081, -0.2228, -0.008, -0.0036, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0013, -0.0016, -0.0033, -0.006, -0.014, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0013, -0.002, -0.0001, -0.0114, -0.3545, -0.0051, -0.0011, -0.0043, -0.0094, -0.3306, -0.24, -0.0542, -0.0027, -0.0002, -0.0119, -0.581, -0.0059, -0.0057, -0.5199, -0.0084, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0136, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0013, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0013, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.0026, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0017, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0005], "cumulative_logprob": -6.1674, "norm_logp": -0.0642, "input_len": 334, "output_tokens": 96, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.46, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "452", "pred": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while as craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "raw_output": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while as craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression made due to volcanic activity, while a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano that allows for the eruption of magma and lava. The primary difference between the two is the method of formation. Calderas are formed when a magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large depression, while craters are formed by impacts from meteorites.", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.903, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 54, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 320, 266, 3245, 825, 13136, 3941, 701, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 1393, 438, 1560, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 11, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4024, -0.0755, -0.0004, -0.0313, -0.3122, -0.0203, -1.5601, -0.002, -0.0012, -0.0339, -0.0211, -0.0013, -0.0433, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0067, -0.0041, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0178, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.1919, -0.1136, -0.5906, -0.8889, -0.0005, -0.0131, -0.0247, -0.0003, -0.0019, -0.0008, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.1846, -0.0095, -0.017, -0.0056, -0.0018, -0.002