{"id": "492", "pred": "By using a website which is able to check the popular tweets which favorite as well as people who favorite your tweets.", "raw_output": "By using a website which is able to check the popular tweets which favorite as well as people who favorite your tweets.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 5.023, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 23, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1359, 1667, 264, 3910, 892, 374, 2952, 311, 1779, 279, 5411, 23150, 892, 6930, 438, 1632, 438, 1251, 879, 6930, 697, 23150, 13], "logp_list": [-1.7138, -0.5236, -0.3438, -0.1204, -0.8167, -0.5125, -0.0077, -0.0024, -0.0257, -0.5032, -0.076, -0.0088, -0.1277, -0.5861, -0.027, -0.0022, -0.0154, -0.0229, -0.1681, -0.0569, -0.1124, -0.0073, -0.1039], "cumulative_logprob": -5.8845, "norm_logp": -0.2558, "input_len": 429, "output_tokens": 23, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 16.98, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 3.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "497", "pred": "Salary exchange is a way of making pension contributions that could allow both employers and employees to save money. An employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in", "raw_output": "Answer: Salary exchange is a way of making pension contributions that could allow both employers and employees to save money. An employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that salary exchange is a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. The salary that is waived is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution, minus any additional contributions. The employer then pays the corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to potentially save money through reduced national insurance contributions.", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.153, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 35, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 41758, 9289, 374, 264, 1616, 315, 3259, 27681, 19026, 429, 1410, 2138, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 1527, 9364, 32908, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304], "logp_list": [-0.8257, -0.1094, -0.2095, -0.1931, -0.5099, -0.0105, -0.1741, -0.1425, -0.0069, -0.0014, -0.0012, -0.4035, -0.0821, -0.0031, -0.0006, -0.0036, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0069, -0.0018, -0.0506, -1.6597, -0.0018, -0.0361, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0846, -0.029, -0.0005, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.007, -0.0008, -0.0098], "cumulative_logprob": -4.5692, "norm_logp": -0.1305, "input_len": 467, "output_tokens": 35, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "507", "pred": "The characteristics of the tropical rainforest floor include the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow on the floor, as well as various types of", "raw_output": "The characteristics of the tropical rainforest floor include the presence of herbs and mushrooms that grow on the floor, as well as various types of", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the characteristics of the tropical rainforest floor are:\n\n1. High levels of rainfall, with some areas receiving up to 400 inches of rain annually.\n2. High humidity due to the large amount of rainfall.\n3. Abundant vegetation, including tall trees, vines, ferns, herbs, mushrooms, fruits, and nuts.\n4. Quick decay of plant and animal matter due to the heat and moisture, which provides rich nutrients for other plants to grow.\n5. Presence of bananas growing on vines.\n\nTherefore, the tropical rainforest floor is characterized by high rainfall, humidity, and lush vegetation, with a diverse array of plant species and a rapid rate of decay and nutrient cycling.", "question": "what are the characteristics of tropical rainforest floor", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.278, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 27, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 17452, 315, 279, 34048, 11174, 50655, 6422, 2924, 279, 9362, 315, 41293, 323, 47151, 429, 3063, 389, 279, 6422, 11, 438, 1632, 438, 5257, 4494, 315], "logp_list": [-0.4493, -0.4266, -0.0014, -0.2281, -0.011, -0.0005, -0.0099, -0.0092, -0.1119, -0.8695, -0.3031, -0.0005, -1.2698, -0.0016, -0.0034, -1.1532, -0.0043, -0.0887, -0.074, -0.0907, -0.3425, -0.7396, -0.0031, -0.0018, -0.0999, -0.0192, -0.0], "cumulative_logprob": -6.3128, "norm_logp": -0.2338, "input_len": 424, "output_tokens": 27, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "516", "pred": "The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle's Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occult", "raw_output": "The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle's Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occult", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "question": "history of the word gemini", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 6.199, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 31, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 1156, 3881, 5785, 315, 279, 85711, 82386, 572, 304, 77901, 594, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 13, 1260, 33845, 429, 566, 13166, 458, 73826], "logp_list": [-0.4654, -0.6985, -0.0161, -0.001, -0.1853, -0.0092, -0.0168, -0.0003, -0.0209, -0.1101, -0.0039, -0.0655, -0.027, -0.0421, -0.0807, -0.0501, -0.1141, -0.0193, -0.0738, -0.1087, -0.0014, -0.0072, -0.0015, -0.0194, -0.9042, -0.0055, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0006], "cumulative_logprob": -3.0502, "norm_logp": -0.0984, "input_len": 338, "output_tokens": 31, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 19.35, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "521", "pred": "The reverse raffle is a raffle but backwards. Your entry ticket has a number on it from 1 to approximately 55. Tickets are $50 each and a table consists of 20 individuals, which constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. and two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pull. individuals totaling twenty.", "raw_output": "The reverse raffle is a raffle but backwards. Your entry ticket has a number on it from 1 to approximately 55. Tickets are $50 each and a table consists of 20 individuals, which constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. and two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pull. individuals totaling twenty.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA raffle is a game of chance where participants buy tickets with a unique number, and a prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket drawn at random. In a reverse raffle, the process is similar, but the tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, and tables consist of 20 individuals, constituting one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes available. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% going to a charitable cause.\n\nTherefore, raffles typically involve buying tickets with unique numbers, and a random draw determines the winner, with a percentage of the proceeds often going to a charitable cause.", "question": "how do raffles work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 16.608, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 82, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 9931, 435, 44090, 374, 264, 435, 44090, 714, 28412, 13, 4615, 4343, 11727, 702, 264, 1372, 389, 432, 504, 220, 16, 311, 13187, 220, 20, 20, 13, 35615, 525, 400, 20, 15, 1817, 323, 264, 1965, 17167, 315, 220, 17, 15, 7775, 11, 892, 41575, 825, 4583, 11727, 518, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 13, 323, 1378, 400, 20, 15, 15, 37408, 320, 603, 369, 279, 4124, 11958, 13330, 323, 825, 369, 279, 1156, 11727, 6815, 13, 7775, 81123, 17073, 13], "logp_list": [-1.641, -0.514, -0.0015, -0.0005, -0.7915, -0.0226, -0.1065, -0.0014, -0.0877, -0.0056, -0.2979, -0.125, -0.0021, -0.0033, -0.017, -0.0319, -0.0003, -0.0015, -0.0003, -0.0014, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0117, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.0126, -0.0677, -0.0045, -0.0098, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.0006, -0.0145, -0.0018, -0.0006, -0.0028, -0.0008, -0.0032, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0052, -0.0188, -0.0015, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0018, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0911, -0.0262, -0.0018, -0.0095, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0114, -0.0026, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.2708, -0.0003, -0.0019, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0311, -0.162, -0.1275, -0.0079, -0.0021, -0.0506], "cumulative_logprob": -4.6185, "norm_logp": -0.0563, "input_len": 353, "output_tokens": 82, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.26, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "530", "pred": "They charge a certain amount from your credit card to establish a balance (it's $100 for the EZ-Tag, $25 for the TX-Tag). They deduct the tolls from that balance till the balance goes below a certain value ($10 for the TX-Tag) and", "raw_output": "They charge a certain amount from your credit card to establish a balance (it's $100 for the EZ-Tag, $25 for the TX-Tag). They deduct the tolls from that balance till the balance goes below a certain value ($10 for the TX-Tag) and", "gt": "Sure! Based on the given passages, here is how E-ZPass charging works:\n\nWhen you sign up for E-ZPass, a certain amount is charged to your credit card to establish a balance (specifically, $100 for the EZ-Tag and $25 for the TX-Tag). This balance is used to pay for tolls as you drive through toll lanes. When you drive through a toll booth, the toll tag mounted on your windshield is read by the antennae, and your E-ZPass account is charged the appropriate amount. The amount is deducted from your balance. If your balance falls below a certain threshold ($10 for the TX-Tag), E-ZPass will charge your credit card an additional $100 or $25 to top up your balance.", "question": "how does ez pass charges work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.09, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 61, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [6865, 6757, 264, 3654, 3311, 504, 697, 6668, 3701, 311, 5695, 264, 8172, 320, 275, 594, 400, 16, 15, 15, 369, 279, 63286, 12, 5668, 11, 400, 17, 20, 369, 279, 17031, 12, 5668, 568, 2379, 36810, 279, 25851, 82, 504, 429, 8172, 11956, 279, 8172, 5780, 3685, 264, 3654, 897, 1711, 16, 15, 369, 279, 17031, 12, 5668, 8, 323], "logp_list": [-1.8551, -0.2023, -0.2009, -0.0066, -0.0001, -0.0074, -0.0149, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.047, -0.0005, -0.0017, -0.0003, -0.6729, -0.0032, -0.0048, -0.0071, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.