{"id": "296", "pred": "Examples of Potential Energy and Kinetic Energy. Potential energy is that energy stored in any body whereas kinetic energy is the energy a body possesses by its motion. We see many a activities across us like a car moves, a ball rolls, river flows, an athlete runs, rides in amusement park, a satellite in orbit etc.", "raw_output": "Examples of Potential Energy and Kinetic Energy. Potential energy is that energy stored in any body whereas kinetic energy is the energy a body possesses by its motion. We see many a activities across us like a car moves, a ball rolls, river flows, an athlete runs, rides in amusement park, a satellite in orbit etc.", "gt": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which can have a large amount of kinetic energy due to its fast velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy. Other activities like a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, rides in an amusement park, and a satellite in orbit also involve potential and kinetic energy.", "question": "what are examples of potential and kinetic energy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 17.913, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 65, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [40381, 315, 53957, 12354, 323, 30891, 5298, 12354, 13, 53957, 4802, 374, 429, 4802, 9768, 304, 894, 2487, 19853, 70323, 4802, 374, 279, 4802, 264, 2487, 49226, 553, 1181, 11379, 13, 1205, 1490, 1657, 264, 7488, 3941, 601, 1075, 264, 1803, 10797, 11, 264, 4935, 27373, 11, 14796, 27455, 11, 458, 33780, 8473, 11, 31227, 304, 61368, 6118, 11, 264, 23210, 304, 26507, 4992, 13], "logp_list": [-0.4453, -0.0165, -1.0925, -0.0687, -1.2117, -0.0052, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.73, -0.1346, -0.0098, -0.0041, -0.0284, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0003, -0.006, -0.0017, -0.0225, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0025, -0.0002, -0.0112, -0.0002, -0.0091, -0.0035, -0.0021, -0.0001, -0.0154, -0.3482, -0.0017, -0.0015, -0.1167, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0017, -0.0013, -0.0002, -0.0658, -0.4847, -0.0262, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0195, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.0032, -0.0001, -0.0055, -0.0016, -0.0004, -0.0013, -0.0163, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0473, -0.0368], "cumulative_logprob": -5.0122, "norm_logp": -0.0771, "input_len": 285, "output_tokens": 65, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "300", "pred": "A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. A pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot.", "raw_output": "A hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. A pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot.", "gt": "To make a hot water heater circulate, one can install a recirculation system. The system consists of a pump that pulls hot water from the water heater and simultaneously sends cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. This ensures that hot water is always available without having to wait for it to circulate naturally. Recirculation systems can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 8.282, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 38, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 4017, 3015, 34755, 13834, 21308, 7010, 3015, 304, 279, 31175, 1182, 311, 279, 3015, 42238, 1526, 264, 470, 1555, 13, 362, 13834, 4225, 23156, 419, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 438, 4362, 311, 2506, 432, 4017, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3636, -0.1354, -0.0013, -0.1394, -0.0008, -0.0307, -0.0047, -0.0001, -0.023, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0246, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.1224, -0.5183, -0.0003, -0.005, -0.0001, -0.1771, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0204], "cumulative_logprob": -2.5766, "norm_logp": -0.0678, "input_len": 298, "output_tokens": 38, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.62, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "304", "pred": "The watermelon explodes due", "raw_output": "The watermelon explodes due", "gt": "Watermelons can explode due to external pressure, such as when a bunch of rubber bands are placed around the middle of the watermelon, causing potential energy to build up and eventually cause the watermelon to explode. Another cause of watermelon explosions is the buildup of gas inside the watermelon due to rotting. This increases the internal pressure and when the skin of the watermelon can no longer hold the gas inside, it will explode.", "question": "what causes watermelons to explode", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 1.375, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 5, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 3015, 71118, 93913, 4152], "logp_list": [-1.6804, -0.7023, -0.0052, -0.1404, -0.1162], "cumulative_logprob": -2.6445, "norm_logp": -0.5289, "input_len": 272, "output_tokens": 5, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "320", "pred": "The effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of your lifestyle. Everything you do has an impact --- positive or negative --- on the environment. That's the concept behind your carbon footprint", "raw_output": "The effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of your lifestyle. Everything you do has an impact --- positive or negative --- on the environment. That's the concept behind your carbon footprint", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that the effect of a carbon footprint is a measure of the negative impact of human activities on the environment. The measurement of a carbon footprint typically indicates the amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases produced through daily activities. Additionally, reducing one's carbon footprint can have a positive impact on the environment, even if the changes may seem small.", "question": "what is the effect of carbon footprint", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.743, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 38, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 2456, 315, 12499, 42872, 374, 311, 6629, 279, 12158, 5421, 315, 697, 18899, 13, 20094, 498, 653, 702, 458, 5421, 12448, 6785, 476, 8225, 12448, 389, 279, 4573, 13, 2938, 594, 279, 7286, 4815, 697, 12499, 42872], "logp_list": [-0.2729, -0.5008, -0.0012, -0.4579, -0.0008, -0.2654, -0.7186, -0.0289, -0.0056, -0.0437, -0.001, -0.0013, -0.7877, -0.0014, -0.2513, -0.9309, -0.0102, -0.0009, -0.2073, -0.0083, -0.0009, -1.0151, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0683, -1.0988, -0.0101, -0.0018, -0.0, -0.0034, -0.0114, -0.0002, -0.0], "cumulative_logprob": -6.7085, "norm_logp": -0.1765, "input_len": 367, "output_tokens": 38, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.38, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "329", "pred": "Boil until tender, then transfer to an ice water bath. When cool, peel off skin. (15 minutes to cook, 5 seconds to peel each cooked potato)", "raw_output": "Boil until tender, then transfer to an ice water bath. When cool, peel off skin. (15 minutes to cook, 5 seconds to peel each cooked potato)", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how to boil potatoes for easy peeling:\n\nPassage 1 recommends boiling the potatoes until they are tender, then transferring them to an ice water bath to stop the cooking process. After cooling, the skin can be easily peeled off. This method takes 15 minutes to cook the potatoes and 5 seconds to peel each one.\n\nPassage 2 suggests peeling the potatoes before cooking, as their skin can be tough and papery. However, no specific instructions are provided for peeling.\n\nPassage 3 provides a step-by-step guide for peeling cooked potatoes. The method involves scoring the potatoes all the way around the center, boiling them for about 15 minutes, then placing them in an ice water bath to cool. Once cooled, the skin can be easily removed. This method takes about 30 minutes total time.\n\nTherefore, based on the provided passages, the best way to boil potatoes for easy peeling is to boil them until tender, then transfer them to an ice water bath to stop the cooking process and make the skin easier to remove. Scoring the potatoes before boiling can also help loosen the skin for easier peeling.", "question": "how to boil potatoes for easy peeling", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.614, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1233, 321, 3080, 27582, 11, 1221, 8317, 311, 458, 9853, 3015, 8885, 13, 3197, 7010, 11, 57112, 1007, 6787, 13, 320, 16, 20, 4420, 311, 4296, 11, 220, 20, 6486, 311, 57112, 1817, 29105, 38734, 8], "logp_list": [-1.0567, -0.0124, -0.7014, -0.0123, -0.1246, -0.017, -0.0028, -0.0004, -0.0008, -0.0009, -0.0018, -0.0022, -0.0239, -0.0744, -0.0034, -0.0018, -0.0011, -0.0046, -0.0036, -0.0532, -1.1633, -0.0021, -0.0002, -0.0018, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.0028, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0024, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0008, -0.0005, -0.0538], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3304, "norm_logp": -0.0925, "input_len": 364, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.5, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "335", "pred": "Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick. Pull on the tick gently and steadily.", "raw_output": "Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick. Pull on the tick gently and steadily.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how to get rid of ticks from dogs:\n\n1. Check inside and behind the dog's ears and around its eyes, which are common hiding places for ticks. (Passage 1)\n2. Use tick control products recommended by a veterinarian to prevent ticks from attaching to the dog. (Passage 2)\n3. Remove ticks from the dog using a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. (Passage 1)\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages whether there are any specific products or methods for getting rid of ticks from dogs that are already infested.", "question": "how to get rid of ticks from dogs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.404, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 43, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [10253, 264, 6716, 315, 14545, 10125, 388, 311, 33377, 279, 1968, 315, 279, 9341, 1380, 432, 84503, 311, 279, 6787, 13, 41062, 35416, 421, 498, 3119, 311, 990, 697, 19225, 311, 4057, 279, 9341, 13, 31828, 389, 279, 9341, 29273, 323, 41971, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3101, -0.0523, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0176, -0.0068, -0.0013, -0.0053, -0.0275, -0.0101, -0.0025, -0.0302, -0.0031, -0.0102, -0.0075, -0.0046, -0.0018, -0.1352, -0.2894, -0.0031, -0.1318, -0.0044, -0.0027, -0.0025, -0.0062, -0.0068, -0.0031, -0.0013, -0.0029, -0.0029, -0.0016, -0.0566, -0.0466, -0.002, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0019, -0.0014, -0.0015, -0.0493], "cumulative_logprob": -2.2466, "norm_lo