{"id": "415", "pred": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Sing Sing Monsters, you need to use the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.", "raw_output": "To breed a Shugabeats in My Sing Sing Monsters, you need to use the Shugabush and the Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's what we know about breeding a Shugabeats in My Singing Monsters:\n\n* We need to breed a Shugabush Monster with a Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure to get a Shugabeats.\n* The breeding combination for Shugabeats is Shugabush + Furcorn.\n* Breeding left or right does not matter.\n\nTherefore, to breed a Shugabeats, you need to have a Shugabush Monster and a Furcorn, and then breed them together in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.\n\nAnswer: Breed a Shugabush Monster and a Furcorn in the Shugabush Island breeding structure.", "question": "how do i breed a shugabeats in my singing monsters", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 13.483, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 27775, 264, 1417, 768, 8229, 1862, 304, 3017, 10833, 10833, 62708, 11, 498, 1184, 311, 990, 279, 1417, 768, 370, 1116, 323, 279, 49840, 38630, 304, 279, 1417, 768, 370, 1116, 10720, 39208, 5944, 13], "logp_list": [-0.1961, -0.0013, -0.0409, -0.0033, -0.0058, -0.0041, -0.0867, -0.1115, -0.0484, -0.0001, -0.1824, -0.0037, -0.026, -0.0994, -0.4687, -0.0204, -1.1267, -0.0188, -0.0011, -0.0065, -0.0009, -0.0044, -0.0181, -0.0118, -0.0128, -0.0015, -0.013, -0.0028, -0.006, -0.0025, -0.0011, -0.0027, -0.0036, -0.0009, -0.0015, -0.4262], "cumulative_logprob": -2.9617, "norm_logp": -0.0823, "input_len": 420, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "425", "pred": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other", "raw_output": "The Universal Product Code (UPC) is a barcode symbology that is widely used in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, in Europe and other", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, an UPDescription is:\n\n1. A barcode symbology used for tracking trade items in stores, consisting of 12 numeric digits that are uniquely assigned to each trade item. (Passage 1)\n2. A process involving receiving a specimen bottle from a member, ensuring it contains a minimum of 30 milliliters of urine, initialing the label in the member's presence, and verifying the information on the label and DD Form 2624 (a military form). (Passage 2)\n3. A barcode partner that provides GS1 Originated UPC and EAN codes, as well as a toll-free number and positive customer reviews. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "what is a upc description", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 7.342, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 36, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 25500, 5643, 6119, 320, 3124, 34, 8, 374, 264, 49407, 66654, 2449, 429, 374, 13570, 1483, 304, 279, 3639, 4180, 11, 6864, 11, 3639, 15072, 11, 8330, 11, 1532, 16911, 11, 304, 4505, 323, 1008], "logp_list": [-1.153, -0.4727, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0725, -0.002, -0.0024, -0.021, -0.0555, -0.0096, -0.0029, -0.0032, -0.0, -1.1307, -0.0105, -0.0075, -0.0003, -0.0625, -0.0587, -0.0064, -0.0008, -0.0049, -0.0005, -0.0012, -0.0097, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0005, -0.001, -0.0134, -0.0003, -0.0195, -0.0898], "cumulative_logprob": -3.2154, "norm_logp": -0.0893, "input_len": 390, "output_tokens": 36, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.79, "GAVIE-Rel": 1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 2.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "430", "pred": "Twins can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs.", "raw_output": "Twins can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how twins happen:\n\n* Monoamniotic twins: These twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization.\n* Conjoined twins: When the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs, it happens within 8 days of fertilization, and mortality is highest for conjoined twins due to the many complications resulting from shared organs.\n* Dizygotic twin pregnancies: These twins are slightly more likely to occur in women who are of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the ages of 30 and 40, are taller and heavier than average, and have had several previous pregnancies.\n* Identical twins (monozygotic): These twins develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos.\n* Fraternal twins (dizygotic): These twins develop from two different eggs, and each twin is fertilized by its own sperm cell.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages: The passage does not provide information on the exact timing or mechanism of twinning beyond what is mentioned above.", "question": "how do twins happen", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.445, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 48, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 387, 2987, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 701, 7290, 429, 807, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 11, 892, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 11, 476, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 701, 7290, 429, 807, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 13], "logp_list": [-0.0546, -0.0, -0.5958, -0.8335, -0.0235, -0.0064, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1576, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.9025, -0.1192, -0.0346, -0.0017, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.004, -0.2746, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.071, -0.0812, -0.0029, -0.0019, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0004, -0.0103, -0.0009, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.004, -0.0082, -0.1016, -0.0, -0.008, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0245], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3339, "norm_logp": -0.0695, "input_len": 349, "output_tokens": 48, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.17, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "434", "pred": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "raw_output": "Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to clean tarnished jewelry at home\":\n\nTo clean tarnished jewelry at home, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry gently, then rinse it in cool water and buff it with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth. You can also use a saltwater solution by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerging the jewelry in it. Let it sit for a while, then remove and rinse it with cool water. Be sure to use a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds.", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.975, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 76, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [58083, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 568], "logp_list": [-2.2127, -0.167, -0.1472, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0053, -0.0018, -0.0091, -0.0031, -0.0002, -0.2067, -0.0209, -0.1891, -0.0017, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.2367, -0.0468, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0372, -0.006, -0.0002, -0.0041, -0.0041, -0.0004, -0.0044, -0.0019, -0.0016, -0.0015, -0.0013, -0.0095, -0.0006, -0.0227, -0.5563, -0.0379, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0037, -0.0118, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0065, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0023, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0622, -0.0155, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0018, -0.0002, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0029, -0.0009, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.7873, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0062, -0.002, -0.0002, -0.0012, -0.0594], "cumulative_logprob": -4.9278, "norm_logp": -0.0648, "input_len": 349, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.14, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "439", "pred": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "raw_output": "To find someone in France, you can visit www.pagesjaunes.fr, the Pages Blanches, which is the French version of the white pages. Enter the person's last name in the Nom field. You can enter a first name in P", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to find someone in France:\n\n1. Use a people search website specifically designed for France, such as People Search France.\n2. Check the French white pages (in English) at PagesJaunes.fr. Instead of typing Paris in the City field, write IDF (or Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field to search for the person in Paris and its suburbs.\n3. Post a missed connection (MC) on Paris Craigslist.\n4. Visit the Pages Blanches (the French version of the white pages) at PagesJaunes.fr. Enter the last name of the person you're searching for in the Nom field, and optionally enter a first name in the Prenom field. Enter address information, if known, in the Ou section. Click Trouver to search for the name. The search results will display name, address, and phone number for all listings that match your search criteria.", "question": "how to find someone in france", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 15.653, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 1, "n_tokens": 49, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1477, 4325, 304, 9625, 11, 498, 646, 3947, 8438, 37980, 5580, 8531, 18798, 11, 279, 21772, 2502, 82181, 11, 892, 374, 279, 8585, 2319, 315, 279, 4158, 6816, 13, 11252, 279, 1697, 594, 1537, 829, 304, 279, 36900, 2070, 13, 1446, 646, 3725, 264, 1156, 829, 304, 393], "logp_list": [-0.3293, -0.0043, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0229, -0.223, -0.0252, -1.6119, -0.7