{"id": "548", "pred": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. It is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike", "raw_output": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. It is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike", "gt": "A transistor is a miniature electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. As an amplifier, it takes a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a larger electric current (output current) at the other end. As a switch, it uses a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a larger supply of electricity. The transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base. The amount of collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 17.17, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 84, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 576, 31953, 1482, 374, 13187, 33218, 320, 5464, 36512, 3248, 1482, 8722, 8, 3039, 279, 2331, 1482, 13, 1084, 374, 11136, 7046, 1091, 220, 16, 15, 15, 369, 2613, 1331, 25719, 1356, 380, 1087, 714, 646, 387, 9155, 304, 1356, 380, 1087, 6188, 369, 1550, 26526, 8357, 13, 26048], "logp_list": [-0.121, -0.0024, -0.0332, -0.0456, -0.2576, -0.0035, -0.2278, -0.011, -0.0013, -0.0054, -0.001, -0.0089, -0.0006, -0.0301, -0.0005, -0.0041, -0.1611, -0.2916, -0.6024, -0.0494, -0.0001, -0.0074, -0.001, -0.0103, -0.0016, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0028, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1362, -0.8946, -0.3859, -0.0011, -0.0312, -0.0057, -0.0095, -0.0821, -0.002, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.4678, -0.0589, -0.0323, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0013, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0037, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0202, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0083, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.107, -0.1315], "cumulative_logprob": -4.2741, "norm_logp": -0.0509, "input_len": 413, "output_tokens": 84, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 37.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "562", "pred": "Global egalitarianism is a free-standing conception of global justice that argues neither equality of resources nor equality of opportunity represents a workable principle of global justice. It suggests that neither can measure the resources or opportunities available to people in different societies in a way that is neutral as between cultures, and such neutrality seems indispensable in a global", "raw_output": "Global egalitarianism is a free-standing conception of global justice that argues neither equality of resources nor equality of opportunity represents a workable principle of global justice. It suggests that neither can measure the resources or opportunities available to people in different societies in a way that is neutral as between cultures, and such neutrality seems indispensable in a global", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer based on the three provided passages:\n\nGlobal egalitarianism refers to the idea that all individuals should have equal rights and opportunities regardless of their background, location, or culture. However, the passages suggest that achieving global egalitarianism is challenging due to difficulties in measuring resources and opportunities across different cultures. Additionally, the passages hint that there may be different types of egalitarianism, such as instrumental and non-instrumental, but do not provide further explanation or clarification. Therefore, without more information, it is difficult to give a more specific definition of global egalitarianism based solely on these passages.", "question": "global egalitarianism meaning", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 13.003, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 65, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [11646, 76171, 20030, 2142, 374, 264, 1910, 55603, 42556, 315, 3644, 12161, 429, 28533, 13866, 21777, 315, 4963, 6329, 21777, 315, 6638, 10868, 264, 975, 480, 17508, 315, 3644, 12161, 13, 1084, 13230, 429, 13866, 646, 6629, 279, 4963, 476, 10488, 2500, 311, 1251, 304, 2155, 33675, 304, 264, 1616, 429, 374, 20628, 438, 1948, 26735, 11, 323, 1741, 51764, 4977, 63184, 304, 264, 3644], "logp_list": [-0.6315, -0.3146, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.6891, -0.1344, -1.2465, -0.0014, -0.0008, -0.0035, -0.0019, -0.0001, -0.1747, -0.6276, -1.454, -0.0015, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0015, -0.035, -0.0001, -0.0028, -0.6543, -0.0023, -0.0556, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.1521, -0.0039, -0.0001, -0.1048, -0.6736, -1.0579, -0.0271, -0.8968, -0.2578, -0.5355, -0.2029, -0.0019, -0.0036, -0.0001, -0.0156, -0.0169, -0.0069, -0.01, -0.0022, -0.0008, -0.0219, -0.0015, -0.0325, -0.0069, -0.0071, -0.0007, -0.1351, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.1611, -0.0483, -0.043, -0.0006, -0.019, -0.0012, -0.0134, -0.0008, -0.0019], "cumulative_logprob": -10.5035, "norm_logp": -0.1616, "input_len": 351, "output_tokens": 65, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 15.24, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "574", "pred": "To quickly get rid of mice, you need to take immediate action. This is a", "raw_output": "To quickly get rid of mice, you need to take immediate action. This is a", "gt": "To quickly get rid of mice, it is important to take immediate action as they can quickly infest your home. One effective method is to seal off any entry points that mice can use to enter your house, such as pipes and vents. Placing steel wool inside these openings before sealing them can prevent mice from reentering. Additionally, eliminating any potential food sources and shelter can help deter mice from your home.", "question": "how to quickly get rid of mice", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 3.594, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 17, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1249, 6157, 633, 9279, 315, 23627, 11, 498, 1184, 311, 1896, 13922, 1917, 13, 1096, 374, 264], "logp_list": [-1.4829, -0.0525, -0.0563, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0268, -0.9429, -0.1799, -0.0017, -0.0215, -0.0013, -0.0016, -0.5001, -0.7367, -0.0734, -0.0284], "cumulative_logprob": -4.1065, "norm_logp": -0.2416, "input_len": 272, "output_tokens": 17, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 21.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "589", "pred": "Form 1099-PATR (Taxable Distributions Received From Cooperatives) should be", "raw_output": "Form 1099-PATR (Taxable Distributions Received From Cooperatives) should be", "gt": "Per the IRS instructions, patronage dividends and other distributions in boxes 1,2,3, and 5 from a cooperative, which are reported in Form 1099-PATR, are reported on Schedule C, Schedule F, or Form 4835 of the Form 1040.", "question": "where does 1099-patr go on 1040", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 4.394, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 21, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1838, 220, 16, 15, 24, 24, 9299, 828, 49, 320, 31349, 480, 422, 17994, 38417, 5542, 23517, 5859, 8, 1265, 387], "logp_list": [-1.2033, -0.0134, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0022, -0.0001, -1.3471, -0.0038, -0.0, -0.4753, -0.008, -0.0131, -0.0087, -0.0006, -0.0027, -0.2485, -0.6887, -0.0937], "cumulative_logprob": -4.111, "norm_logp": -0.1958, "input_len": 279, "output_tokens": 21, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.24, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "601", "pred": "C\nHuman: Read the following text and answer the question.\n\nText: passage", "raw_output": "C\nHuman: Read the following text and answer the question.\n\nText: passage", "gt": "The passages suggest that both cycling and aerobic exercise are beneficial. However, cycling, including stationary cycling, may be particularly ideal for individuals with arthritic or other orthopedic problems who may have difficulty walking for extended periods. Furthermore, stationary biking provides a low impact workout as it supports the majority of your weight. Therefore, while both exercises are valuable, the passages may imply that cycling could be better for those with specific physical conditions.", "question": "which is better exercise cycling or aerobic", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 3.602, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 18, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [34, 198, 151643, 33975, 25, 4457, 279, 2701, 1467, 323, 4226, 279, 3405, 382, 1178, 25, 21085, 220], "logp_list": [-1.4745, -0.0257, -0.0038, -0.1563, -0.0933, -1.8096, -0.0077, -0.1371, -0.2208, -0.0671, -0.0575, -0.0034, -0.0122, -0.3302, -0.1151, -0.0075, -0.4434, -0.0009], "cumulative_logprob": -4.9661, "norm_logp": -0.2759, "input_len": 222, "output_tokens": 18, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "609", "pred": "Make a list of all your employees and the duties they can perform. Make a chart of the work days and the hours for each day. Print out copies for employees or send schedules to employee email accounts. Keep a master schedule for to make any changes. Microsoft Excel", "raw_output": "Make a list of all your employees and the duties they can perform. Make a chart of the work days and the hours for each day. Print out copies for employees or send schedules to employee email accounts. Keep a master schedule for to make any changes. Microsoft Excel", "gt": "To create employee schedules, start by making a list of all employees and the tasks they can perform. Then, create a chart with the work days and hours for each day. You can use scheduling software, a word processing program, or a sheet of paper to do this. Afterward, print out copies for employees or send schedules to their email accounts. It's also important to maintain a master schedule for making any necessary changes. Microsoft Excel can be used to create a work schedule for multiple employees, which can be modified as your business expands.", "question": "how to do employee schedules", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 10.653, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 53, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [8078, 264, 1140, 315, 678, 697, 8256, 323, 279, 21300, 807, 646, 2736, 13, 7405, 264, 9487, 315, 279, 975, 2849, 323, 279, 4115, 369, 1817, 1899, 13, 8213, 700, 10993, 369, 8256, 476, 3624, 36769, 311, 9364, 2551, 9618, 13, 13655, 264, 7341, 9700, 369, 311, 1281, 894, 4344, 13, 5100, 21029], "logp_list": [-1.9307, -0.0039, -0.0499, -0.0042, -0.0922, -0.0254, -0.0009, -0.0052, -0.0048, -0.0001, -0.0031, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.8807, -0.1247, -0.0006, -0.0004, -0.0006, -0.002, -0.0002, -0.0016, -0.0008, -0.0031, -0.0001, -0.0027, -0.0002, -0.0175, -0.0187, -0.6675, -0.0022, -0.0003, -0.0135, -0.001, -0.0134, -0.0019, -0.0015, -0.0013, -0.0092, -0.0012, -0.0004, -0.0041, -0.1026, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.036, -0.0148, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0002, -0.0091, -0.549, -0.0014], "cumulative_logprob": -4.6068, "norm_logp": -0.0869, "input_len": 248, "output_tokens": 53, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "615", "pred": "Step 2: Remove the printer from the devices list. Remove printers and uninstall any Windows print drivers using Windows Devices and Printers. If your printer connects to the computer with a USB cable, disconnect the cable from the printer. Turn the printer off and on again, and", "raw_output": "Step 2: Remove the printer from the devices list. Remove printers and uninstall any Windows print drivers using Windows Devices and Printers. If your printer connects to the computer with a USB cable, disconnect the cable from the printer. Turn the printer off and on again, and", "gt": "Passage 2 provides the necessary information to force removal of a printer in devices and printers. You can do this by first clicking on Control Panel, then selecting Hardware and Sound, followed by clicking on Printers. Right-click on the printer you want to remove and click Delete. If you are unable to delete the printer, right-click it again, click Run as administrator, and then click Delete.", "question": "how to force removal of a printer in devices and printers", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 11.223, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 55, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 17, 25, 10783, 279, 22380, 504, 279, 7611, 1140, 13, 10783, 55953, 323, 53635, 894, 5515, 1173, 11788, 1667, 5515, 40377, 323, 8213, 388, 13, 1416, 697, 22380, 33061, 311, 279, 6366, 448, 264, 11348, 14652, 11, 28969, 279, 14652, 504, 279, 22380, 13, 11999, 279, 22380, 1007, 323, 389, 1549, 11, 323], "logp_list": [-2.109, -0.0119, -0.569, -0.0206, -0.1613, -0.0118, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0017, -0.0016, -0.0002, -0.0236, -0.1362, -0.0005, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0081, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0017, -0.0061, -0.0015, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0075, -0.0947, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0017, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0013, -0.0092, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0013, -0.0002], "cumulative_logprob": -3.1887, "norm_logp": -0.058, "input_len": 263, "output_tokens": 55, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.66, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "625", "pred": "Technology has dramatically reshaped the average workplace in America over the", "raw_output": "Technology has dramatically reshaped the average workplace in America over the", "gt": "Based on the given passages, technology has changed the workplace in several ways, including:\n\n1. Increased efficiency and productivity: Technology has enabled automation of many tasks, allowing employees to focus on more complex and creative work, leading to increased productivity.\n2. Changes in workplace dynamics: Technology has transformed the way people communicate and collaborate in the workplace, enabling remote work and virtual teams.\n3. Rapid advancements: Technology has rapidly advanced in recent years, allowing employees to access new tools and resources, and adapt to changing market conditions.\n4. Global connectivity: Technology has enabled employees to interact with a global market and work remotely, allowing for greater flexibility and mobility.\n\nTherefore, technology has had a significant impact on the modern workplace, transforming the way people work, communicate, and collaborate.", "question": "how has it technology changed the workplace", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 2.559, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 12, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [62407, 702, 27957, 63693, 10185, 279, 5461, 26368, 304, 5159, 916, 279], "logp_list": [-0.4502, -0.0288, -0.2778, -0.0203, -0.0003, -0.0075, -0.007, -0.0003, -0.0312, -0.0101, -0.3326, -0.0009], "cumulative_logprob": -1.167, "norm_logp": -0.0972, "input_len": 251, "output_tokens": 12, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.77, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "630", "pred": "Stay as close as possible to a tall, skilled shooter when you guard him near the three-point line. Actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities", "raw_output": "Answer: Stay as close as possible to a tall, skilled shooter when you guard him near the three-point line. Actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to guard a tall person in basketball:\n\n1. Stay as close as possible to the tall, skilled shooter when guarding them near the three-point line.\n2. Actively slide your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize shot opportunities.\n3. Keep a wedge between the basket and the offensive player to defend against a tall player.\n\nTherefore, to guard a tall person in basketball, it is essential to stay close to them, move quickly laterally to maintain position, and use a defensive strategy such as keeping a wedge between the basket and the offensive player.", "question": "how to guard a tall person in basketball", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 8.169, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 41, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 28737, 438, 3265, 438, 3204, 311, 264, 16217, 11, 25530, 31571, 979, 498, 7616, 1435, 3143, 279, 2326, 16574, 1555, 13, 3218, 3132, 14983, 697, 7541, 504, 3108, 311, 3108, 311, 4717, 3143, 697, 14704, 323, 29337, 6552, 10488], "logp_list": [-0.8378, -0.0086, -0.3835, -0.1138, -0.0004, -0.0024, -0.0002, -0.0041, -0.0157, -0.001, -0.0196, -0.0001, -0.0012, -0.2426, -0.0033, -0.0024, -0.0052, -0.0015, -0.0004, -0.0015, -0.0043, -0.0001, -0.0369, -0.0162, -0.0001, -0.0009, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0058, -0.0036, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0051, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0005], "cumulative_logprob": -1.7232, "norm_logp": -0.042, "input_len": 247, "output_tokens": 41, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.02, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "634", "pred": "Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of of cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs in the two systems.", "raw_output": "Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of of cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs in the two systems.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the nervous system and endocrine system have some similarities and differences. \n\nSimilarities:\n- Both systems are responsible for maintaining homeostasis in the body.\n- Both systems use chemical messengers to coordinate and regulate the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n- Negative feedback mechanisms are used to regulate both systems.\n\nDifferences:\n- The nervous system is made up of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n- Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses while transmission in the endocrine system is done through chemicals called hormones.\n- The nervous system is a continuous network of interconnected cells while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs that are not physically connected.\n- The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 12.759, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 63, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [20629, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 28229, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 315, 7761, 11, 38781, 11, 35753, 11, 323, 5942, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 11, 7892, 862, 1714, 315, 18368, 323, 4879, 43542, 304, 279, 1378, 5942, 13], "logp_list": [-0.755, -0.1754, -0.0089, -0.0432, -0.0002, -0.3936, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.1266, -0.0059, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0027, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0295, -0.0007, -0.0005, -0.002, -0.0009, -0.0003, -0.0015, -0.4767, -0.0067, -0.0025, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0039, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.036, -0.5818, -0.0011, -0.0191, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0009, -0.0023, -0.0197, -0.2849, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0015, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0022, -0.0273, -0.0039, -0.0003, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.1073, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0016, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0022, -0.0562], "cumulative_logprob": -3.1938, "norm_logp": -0.0507, "input_len": 278, "output_tokens": 63, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "638", "pred": "Pain-killing properties, spicy weight loss alternative, natural nasal decongestant, may help prevent heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, full of vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, cardiovascular health benefits.", "raw_output": "Pain-killing properties, spicy weight loss alternative, natural nasal decongestant, may help prevent heart disease, heart attack, and cancer, full of vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants, cardiovascular health benefits.", "gt": "Jalapenos have health benefits such as pain relief, weight loss, nasal decongestant properties, and potential prevention of heart disease, heart attack, and cancer. They are also rich in vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Additionally, jalapenos can help defend heart health and provide cardiovascular benefits.", "question": "health benefits to jalapenos", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 9.112, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 43, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [47, 466, 12646, 9400, 5888, 11, 47251, 4680, 4709, 10555, 11, 5810, 71088, 409, 443, 6969, 517, 11, 1231, 1492, 5358, 4746, 8457, 11, 4746, 3359, 11, 323, 9387, 11, 2480, 315, 45092, 11, 32972, 11, 323, 80015, 11, 40613, 2820, 7567, 13], "logp_list": [-1.3666, -0.0011, -0.5008, -0.0007, -0.043, -0.0945, -0.3734, -0.0052, -0.0115, -0.021, -0.0034, -0.0772, -0.0035, -0.0018, -0.0008, -0.0037, -0.0011, -0.0061, -0.2757, -0.0921, -0.0109, -0.0062, -0.0035, -0.0498, -0.0709, -0.011, -0.0796, -0.0603, -0.5254, -0.0176, -0.0283, -0.0003, -0.0007, -0.0026, -0.0004, -0.0087, -0.0266, -0.0003, -0.3022, -1.0517, -0.0075, -0.2232, -0.3524], "cumulative_logprob": -5.7233, "norm_logp": -0.1331, "input_len": 279, "output_tokens": 43, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.62, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "643", "pred": "Similarities: - RBCs, WBCs are formed elements of blood. - RBCs, WBCs are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow.", "raw_output": "Similarities: - RBCs, WBCs are formed elements of blood. - RBCs, WBCs are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the primary difference between red and white blood cells is their respective functions within the body. Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery to tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat diseases and viruses. However, both types of cells share some common features, such as being formed elements of blood, being formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow, having a cell membrane and cytoplasm, and circulating in the fluids in blood vessels, which is pumped by the heart.", "question": "differences and similarities between red and white blood cells", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visual": false, "n_image_tokens": 0, "time_sec": 14.616, "status": "success", "n_rollback": 0, "n_tokens": 38, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 25, 481, 431, 4897, 82, 11, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 14122, 5424, 315, 6543, 13, 481, 431, 4897, 82, 11, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 14122, 504, 59117, 573, 63532, 19101, 7761, 304, 2518, 17240, 82648, 13], "logp_list": [-0.3817, -0.0019, -0.087, -0.1265, -0.2616, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0341, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0083, -0.002, -0.0161, -0.0007, -0.0005, -0.0119, -0.0346, -0.003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0244, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0014, -0.0092, -0.0036, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0018, -0.0011, -0.0011, -0.0006, -0.0721], "cumulative_logprob": -1.087, "norm_logp": -0.0286, "input_len": 405, "output_tokens": 38, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.16, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "647", "pred": "Cut large, sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut end can dry out. Small potato can be planted whole. Water the potato thoroughly. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines. Mul", "raw_output": "Answer: Cut large, sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut end can dry out. Small potato can be planted whole. Water the potato thoroughly. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines. Mul", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "opera", "has_visua